WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.072
Welcome back to your How We Made Your Mother podcast feed.
欢迎回到您的《老爸老妈浪漫史》播客节目。

00:00:03.072 --> 00:00:16.000
Today, we're presenting an appearance by our own Craig Thomas on a podcast called Arts Educators Save the World, where successful artists sit in conversation with their mentors to talk about the importance of art, arts education and mentorship.
今天，我们将为您呈现本剧主创克雷格·托马斯（Craig Thomas）在《艺术教育家拯救世界》（Arts Educators Save the World）播客节目中的访谈。在该节目中，杰出的艺术家们会与他们的导师坐在一起，探讨艺术、艺术教育以及导师制的重要性。

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:25.120
I produced this podcast with my lifelong friend Erika, and last week we brought you an episode with Josh Radner and his teacher from NYU.
这个播客是我和我的挚友埃丽卡（Erika）共同制作的，上周我们为您播出了乔什·拉德纳（Josh Radner）与他在纽约大学的老师的访谈。

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:33.880
This week, we've got Craig with Rob Greenberg, an experienced writer and showrunner who helped Craig and Carter in the early years of Hymmium and beyond.
本周，我们的嘉宾是克雷格和罗布·格林伯格（Rob Greenberg）。罗布是一位经验丰富的编剧和剧集运作人，在《老爸老妈浪漫史》早期及之后的岁月里，他给予了克雷格和卡特诸多帮助。

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:46.468
If you enjoyed these episodes, please check out Art's Educators Save the World, where guests have included Lin-Manuel Miranda, Annally Ashford, Cecily Strong, Bradley Whitford, Jonathan graph, Billy Eichner, and many, many more.
如果你喜欢这些节目，请关注《艺术教育家拯救世界》，节目嘉宾包括林-曼努埃尔·米兰达（Lin-Manuel Miranda）、安娜丽·阿什福德（Annally Ashford）、塞西莉·斯特朗（Cecily Strong）、布莱德利·惠特福德（Bradley Whitford）、乔纳森·格罗夫（Jonathan Groff）、比利·艾奇纳（Billy Eichner）等等。

00:00:46.468 --> 00:00:46.968
Enjoy.
请欣赏。

00:00:46.799 --> 00:00:50.144
Writers often get asked, how do you learn how to do that job?
编剧经常会被问到：你是怎么学会做那份工作的？

00:00:50.144 --> 00:00:52.501
Or how do you learn how to be a show miner?
或者，你是怎么学会成为一名剧集运作人的？

00:00:52.501 --> 00:00:58.860
What's interesting about this podcast in general is exploring the different ways that people learn and are mentored.
这个播客总的来说很有意思的一点，就是探索人们学习和接受指导的不同方式。

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:05.603
Such a huge part of what Rob did for us and what I try to do to younger writers now is just telling them they're not crazy.
罗布为我们所做的事情中，有很大一部分——以及我现在试图对年轻编剧做的事情——就是告诉他们：你们没有疯。

00:01:05.603 --> 00:01:10.592
Just being reassured you're not crazy for the weird, unique, specific thing you want to do.
仅仅是得到重新保证：对于你想做的那些古怪、独特、具体的事情，你并没有疯。

00:01:10.592 --> 00:01:15.799
That's the thing you You think is special about the thing you've created is worth holding onto.
这就是你所认为的，你所创造的东西中特别的地方，它值得你坚持下去。

00:01:15.799 --> 00:01:23.920
This is Arts Educators Save the World, where successful artists and their mentors talk about how arts education transformed their lives.
这里是《艺术教育家拯救世界》，杰出的艺术家与他们的导师在这里畅谈艺术教育如何改变了他们的生活。

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:45.180
Hey, Alec, hey everyone, welcome back to Arts Educators Save the World, a podcast where successful artists and their mentors talk about the impact that arts education has had on both of their lives.
嘿，亚历克，大家好，欢迎回到《艺术教育家拯救世界》。这是一个播客节目，杰出的艺术家和他们的导师在这里畅谈艺术教育对他们两人生活的影响。

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:49.340
I'm really excited to be sharing this with you today.
今天能和大家分享这些，我真的非常兴奋。

00:01:49.340 --> 00:02:00.620
We are going to hear from two folks who do something artistically that I know nothing about, which is the art of the show runner.
我们将听到两位艺术创作者的分享，他们所从事的艺术领域我一无所知，那就是剧集运作人的艺术。

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:13.180
If you don't know what a show runner is, be excited to hear from two of television's most successful show runners, Craig Thomas and Rob Greenberg.
如果你不知道什么是剧集运作人，那就准备好聆听电视界最成功的两位剧集运作人——克雷格·托马斯和罗布·格林伯格的分享吧。

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:21.180
I got a chance to hear about how people learn to do an art form that you don't pick up in formal education.
我有机会了解到人们是如何学习一种在正规教育中学不到的艺术形式的。

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:30.120
So these two folks were mentor, mentee, within not only an adult context, but in a professional context.
所以这两位不仅是在成年人的语境下，更是在职业语境下形成了导师与学员的关系。

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:37.120
Rob has been Craig's professional mentor around the art of show running.
在克雷格于2000年代初推出他的热门电视剧《老爸老妈浪漫史》时，罗布一直是他在剧集运作艺术方面的职业导师。

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:44.199
When he launched his hit television series, how I met your mother in the early mid 2000s.
在2000年代初中期，当他推出他的热门电视剧《老爸老妈浪漫史》时。

00:02:44.199 --> 00:02:50.960
Yes indeed, yes, show running is one where until you're just right there seeing it, it is a mystery.
千真万确，没错，剧集运作就是这样一种工作：除非你亲眼目睹，否则它就是一个谜。

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.240
I mean, the short answer as you'll see is it's running.
我的意思是，正如你将看到的，简而言之，这就是运作。

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:58.520
The whole show, you are the last word of what does go on the screen.
运作整部剧，对于什么能最终呈现在屏幕上，你拥有最终决定权。

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:04.879
And so that involves interacting with more than 100 people, much more than 100 people.
所以这涉及与100多人互动，远不止100人。

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:15.240
With no experience prior to doing it, it's like you could run for president and you can say all the things in the world that you've done that make you a good candidate for president, but there's nothing like being president.
在没有任何相关经验的情况下做这件事，就像你要竞选总统一样，你可以列举出世界上所有证明你适合当总统的经历，但没有什么能比得上真正当总统。

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:16.159
And it's the same thing here.
这里也是一样。

00:03:16.159 --> 00:03:21.680
So Craig and I, and this would be, this is obviously my most personal episode here.
所以克雷格和我——显然这是我这里最私密的一期节目。

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:24.000
Craig and I are, he's one of my best friends.
克雷格和我……他是我的至交好友之一。

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.000
We went to school together at Wesleyan.
我们一起在卫斯理大学上学。

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.960
We were housemates and roommates for three or four years, English majors.
我们做过三四年的同屋和室友，都是英语专业。

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:35.039
Then he went off and created how I met your mother without me.
后来他独自去创作了《老爸老妈浪漫史》，没有我。

00:03:35.039 --> 00:03:37.199
I didn't, that part I wasn't involved in.
那部分我没有参与。

00:03:37.199 --> 00:03:43.039
I did get to work for the show for a while, and yeah, this is a great example of how mentorship never ends.
不过我确实为这部剧工作过一段时间，是的，这是一个很好的例子，说明导师制永远不会结束。

00:03:43.039 --> 00:04:13.960
I'd say that's the real takeaway for this one is that, okay, you run a major network television show and 10 million people watch it, but you, the human that has to do that, or in this case, Craig and his co-creator, partner Carter Bays, sure, they're doing it, but they still need to learn stuff as they're doing it, and so we're going to get to hear about him learning it from the great Rob Greenberg.
我想这才是这期节目真正的核心收获：好吧，你运作了一部主流电视网的大热剧集，有1,000万人观看，但你——必须完成这项工作的那个活生生的人，或者在这个例子中，克雷格和他的联合创作人、伙伴卡特·贝斯（Carter Bays）——当然，他们在做这件事，但他们在边做边学的过程中仍然需要学习新东西，所以接下来我们将听到他是如何向伟大的罗布·格林伯格学习这些的。

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:19.680
Well, this week I'm going to start us off here because I'm bringing in a friend of mine for many, many years.
好的，这周由我来开场，因为我请来了一位我认识很多年的老朋友。

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.759
Craig Thomas is a writer and producer and the co-creator of the beloved TV show How I Met Your Mother.
克雷格·托马斯是一位编剧兼制作人，也是备受喜爱的电视剧《老爸老妈浪漫史》的联合创作人。

00:04:25.759 --> 00:04:33.360
As a graduate of Wesleyan University, he follows in the grand tradition of some of our past guests, including Lin-Manuel Miranda and Ted Mosby.
作为卫斯理大学的毕业生，他继承了我们过去某些嘉宾的伟大传统，其中包括林-曼努埃尔·米兰达和泰德·莫斯比（Ted Mosby）。

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.832
And we have another great special Wesleyan guest coming up soon.
不久后我们还将迎来另一位优秀的卫斯理大学特邀嘉宾。

00:04:37.832 --> 00:04:39.160
More on that later.
稍后会有更多相关消息。

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:58.263
After graduation, Craig and his writing partner, Carter Bays, went on to write for the late show at David Letterman and then wrote for the half hour shows, Oliver Bean and American Dad and others before eventually creating How I Met Your Mother, which ran for, correct me if I'm wrong, 208 episodes on CBS from 2005 to 2014.
毕业后，克雷格和他的写作伙伴卡特·贝斯曾为大卫·莱特曼的《深夜秀》撰稿，后来又为半小时情景喜剧《奥利弗·比恩》（Oliver Bean）和《美国老爹》（American Dad）等剧集编剧，之后共同创作了《老爸老妈浪漫史》。如果我没记错的话，该剧于2005年至2014年在CBS播出了208集。

00:04:58.263 --> 00:04:58.763
Correct.
没错。

00:04:58.763 --> 00:04:59.263
Correct.
没错。

00:04:59.263 --> 00:05:00.030
I am correct.
我没记错。

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:04.570
He's recently been publishing prose, which we will link to in the show notes.
他最近一直在出版散文作品，我们会在节目简介中附上链接。

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:11.233
And Craig is a musician and songwriter, has also written for Sesame Street, and I think we can just end it there.
克雷格同时也是一名音乐人和词曲作者，还曾为《芝麻街》写过歌，我想我们就在这里打住吧。

00:05:11.233 --> 00:05:20.666
But it was back in 2005 at the start of How I Met Your Mother, known to many as Hemium, if you hear that come out of our mouths, where Rob will enter our story.
但故事要回到2005年《老爸老妈浪漫史》开播之时——许多人将其称为“Hymmium”，如果你听到我们嘴里冒出这个词——罗布就是在这个时候进入我们的故事的。

00:05:20.666 --> 00:05:26.858
We'll talk about him in just a moment, but Craig, I wonder, first of all, hi, welcome to the show, Craig.
我们稍后会聊到他，但克雷格，首先我想问你，嗨，欢迎来到节目，克雷格。

00:05:26.858 --> 00:05:31.457
Hi, thanks for having me and soon us because Rob will speak at some point too.
嗨，谢谢你们邀请我，也“很快”邀请我们，因为罗布待会儿也会说话。

00:05:31.457 --> 00:05:36.527
Yeah, so Craig, if you could introduce Rob to us, who have you brought with you today?
好的，那么克雷格，你能向我们介绍一下罗布吗？你今天把谁带到了现场？

00:05:36.527 --> 00:05:45.725
I have brought my dear and beloved friend and mentor to me in 2005 and continually mentoring and making my life smarter and better every time I talk to him.
我带来了我亲爱的挚友兼导师。他在2005年指导了我，并且每次我跟他交谈时，都在持续给我指导，让我的生活变得更聪明、更美好。

00:05:45.725 --> 00:06:01.940
Rob Greenberg, who is a phenomenal writer and director, and was at the time of How I Met Your Mother's lunch in 2005, considered the adult as compared to me and Carter Bays, the co-creator of How I Met Your Mother, who were I think eight years old when we launched that show.
罗布·格林伯格。他是一位杰出的编剧兼导演，在2005年《老爸老妈浪漫史》开播时，他被认为是个“成年人”，而相比之下，我和《老爸老妈浪漫史》的另一位联合创作人卡特·贝斯就像是……我觉得我们推出那部剧时只有八岁。

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:02.440
Yeah.
是的。

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.916
And so Rob would help us cross the street.
所以罗布会扶我们过马路。

00:06:04.916 --> 00:06:05.978
He bought us beer.
他给我们买啤酒。

00:06:05.978 --> 00:06:07.982
We drank our first beers with Rob.
我们跟罗布一起喝了生平第一杯啤酒。

00:06:07.982 --> 00:06:15.883
He was our older wiser, not that much older, not that much older, but definitely wiser mentor who'd been around the block a few times.
他是我们年长且睿智的导师——其实也没有大那么多，没有大那么多，但绝对更睿智——他已经在这一行历练过好几次了。

00:06:15.883 --> 00:06:18.360
He had written for Frazier for many years.
他曾为《欢乐一家亲》（Frasier）当过多年编剧。

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:23.077
He had made a bunch of pilots at CBS with a network that how much mother was at.
他在《老爸老妈浪漫史》所在的CBS电视网制作过一堆试播集。

00:06:23.077 --> 00:06:42.063
And he just looked and seemed like a real actual adult as opposed to the mischievous kids visiting Willy Wonka's factory that Carter and I were, I saw a picture of us recently from that time and I can't blame the studio and the network for not trusting a multi-million dollar enterprise to the embryos in that photo I saw.
他看起来和表现得就像一个真正的成年人，而我和卡特则像是误入威利·旺卡工厂的调皮小孩。我最近看到了我们当时的一张照片，我真的不能怪工作室和电视网不把几百万美元的项目托付给照片里那几个胚胎。

00:06:42.063 --> 00:06:44.362
And so Rob was just a wonderful mentor.
所以罗布真的是一位绝佳的导师。

00:06:44.362 --> 00:06:47.015
He is a fantastic writer, fantastic director.
他是一位出色的编剧，出色的导演。

00:06:47.015 --> 00:06:50.376
He has created his own shows, he has directed big movies.
他创作了自己的剧集，执导过大电影。

00:06:50.376 --> 00:07:00.223
He is just someone that I've always looked up to and was pivotal as a mentor and support system in launching the biggest hit I'll ever have, which is how I'm a mother.
他是我一直仰慕的人，在我推出这辈子最大的热门剧集《老爸老妈浪漫史》的过程中，他在导师身份和支持系统上起到了至关重要的作用。

00:07:00.223 --> 00:07:02.346
It's a pleasure to welcome him here.
很高兴能在这里欢迎他。

00:07:02.346 --> 00:07:02.846
Hi, Rob.
嗨，罗布。

00:07:02.846 --> 00:07:04.555
I'll welcome you to the show.
欢迎你来到节目。

00:07:04.555 --> 00:07:06.383
Hi, it's a pleasure to be here.
嗨，很高兴来到这里。

00:07:06.383 --> 00:07:07.680
Now you say something.
现在轮到你说了。

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:10.570
I think you were eight and I was 107 when we met.
我觉得我们见面时你八岁，而我107岁。

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:13.514
Because age is converged over time.
因为年龄随着时间推移而趋同了。

00:07:13.514 --> 00:07:14.495
That's what happens.
事情就是这样。

00:07:14.495 --> 00:07:16.846
So now you're both going in the other direction.
所以现在你们两个都在朝相反的方向发展。

00:07:16.846 --> 00:07:17.826
That's just science.
这就是科学。

00:07:17.826 --> 00:07:19.639
Craig is now five years older than I.
克雷格现在比我大五岁。

00:07:19.639 --> 00:07:23.920
All right, so I'm going to frame this for us here.
好吧，我来为我们梳理一下背景。

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.540
Eric's book, How the Arts Can Save Education is about many things.
埃丽卡的《艺术如何拯救教育》（How the Arts Can Save Education）一书涵盖了许多内容。

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:33.959
But one is that classroom teaching techniques don't need to be siloed away from the rest of the world.
但其中一点是，课堂教学技巧不需要与世界其他地方彻底割裂。

00:07:33.959 --> 00:07:41.360
That teachers can use methods of sharing knowledge and skills in ways that other people do as well.
教师可以采用其他人也在使用的方法来分享知识和技能。

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:48.399
And specifically, she points to the arts and the way artists collaborate through improvisation, through trial and error, and through play.
具体来说，她指出了艺术以及艺术家通过即兴创作、试错和游戏进行合作的方式。

00:07:48.399 --> 00:07:52.560
This is a great way to teach not just art, but math and science and everything else.
这不仅是教授艺术的绝佳方法，也是教授数学、科学和其他一切学科的好方法。

00:07:52.560 --> 00:08:02.040
So the way we're looking at this, I'm looking at this, is that obviously making a television show is a highly collaborative, a constantly collaborative art form.
所以我们看待这件事的方式——我是这样看的——显而易见，制作一部电视剧是一种高度合作、持续合作的艺术形式。

00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:11.199
And Craig, when you and Carter sold the show, you hadn't run a show before, and so Rob was brought in to mentor you kids in the arts.
克雷格，当和卡特把剧卖出去时，你们以前没有运作过剧集，所以罗布被请来指导你们这些在艺术上的年轻人。

00:08:11.199 --> 00:08:12.199
Yeah.
是的。

00:08:12.199 --> 00:08:14.160
So I'm kind of thinking about this as showrunner's school.
所以我把这看作是“剧集运作人学校”。

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:25.600
Craig attended showrunner's school under Rob's mentorship, so let's start with this just so We can kind of set the stage as we connect what went on between the two of you as mentor and Craig.
克雷格在罗布的指导下上了剧集运作人学校，所以让我们从这里开始，以便为你们两人作为导师和学员之间的经历做个铺垫。

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:34.440
So in my attempt to relate what goes on at a big Hollywood studio to what teachers can do in their classroom, let's start off with this.
为了将好莱坞大制片厂发生的事情与老师在课堂上能做的事情联系起来，我们从这里开始吧。

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.870
Craig, what is a showrunner?
克雷格，什么是剧集运作人（showrunner）？

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:50.250
Here's what a showrunner is, and no one teaches you this, and it's impossible, it's the hardest thing to learn as you go, and Rob was this wonderful voice of kind of taking me through it and reducing the panic involved desperately climbing up that learning curve.
剧集运作人就是这样的，没有人教过你这个，而且这是不可能的，这是你边干边学最难掌握的事情，而罗布就是这样一个美妙的声音，他带领我度过这一切，减轻了那种拼命爬上那条学习曲线所带来的绝望恐慌。

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.730
We're 29, Carter and I, when we wrote How I Met Your Mother.
我和卡特写《老爸老妈浪漫史》的时候才29岁。

00:08:52.730 --> 00:09:00.950
And by the way, everything I'm gonna say about Rob and mentorship and learning to be a show winner, I know that Carter would second all of it, especially the loving Rob Greenberg parts.
顺便说一句，关于罗布、导师制以及学习如何成为剧集运作人，我所要说的一切，我知道卡特都会举双手赞成，尤其是那些表达对罗布·格林伯格热爱的部分。

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:04.049
A show runner is, you write a script.
剧集运作人就是，你写了一个剧本。

00:09:04.049 --> 00:09:06.750
Maybe you're lucky enough to have been a writer on a couple of shows.
也许你很幸运，曾在好几部剧里当过编剧。

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:12.409
I had mostly written for late night shows, or one in particular late show with David Letterman.
我大部分时间是为深夜秀写东西，尤其是大卫·莱特曼的《深夜秀》。

00:09:12.409 --> 00:09:19.690
When we wrote the pilot for How I Met Your Mother, I had never even been on staff really for a multi-camera sitcom show.
当我们写《老爸老妈浪漫史》的试播集时，我甚至从来没有真正担任过多机位情景喜剧的编剧工作人员。

00:09:19.690 --> 00:09:25.049
Briefly for one second on this incredibly forgettable show, but I'd written on this animated show, American Dad.
只在一部极其容易被人遗忘的剧里短暂待过一秒钟，但我曾为一部叫《美国老爹》的动画片写过剧本。

00:09:25.049 --> 00:09:31.970
Basically, we were writing our first real live-action multi-camera comedy show and then became showrunners.
基本上，我们是在写我们第一部真正的真人多机位喜剧，然后就直接成了剧集运作人。

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:39.529
So you go from being writers that are introverted, neurotic, crazy, unpresentable people.
所以你从一群内向、神经质、疯狂、无法见人的编剧，摇身一变。

00:09:39.529 --> 00:09:41.570
We should not be presented to the world.
我们本不应该被带到世人面前。

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:43.529
We hide away in our caves.
我们总是躲在自己的山洞里。

00:09:43.529 --> 00:10:00.129
And then there's this thing you become as a showrunner, all of a sudden you're not just a writer, you are the school teacher of a school room full of writers, so all of a sudden you're teaching a staff full of writers that are writing your show, while also being kind of the public face and the business people of the show.
而且当你成为剧集运作人时，你就会变成这样一种人：突然之间你不仅仅是一个编剧，你还是一个塞满编剧的教室里的学校老师，所以你突然开始教导一整个编剧团队来写你的剧，同时还要充当剧集的公众门面和商务负责人。

00:10:00.129 --> 00:10:10.970
You're pitching to the network, you're pitching to the studio, you're trying to present yourself as this competent force of keeping this multi-million dollar operation together when in fact you have no idea what you're doing.
你要向电视网推介，你要向制片厂推介，你要努力把自己塑造成一个有能力把这个数百万美元的项目维系在一起的干练力量，但事实上你根本不知道自己在做什么。

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:33.450
And no one teaches you how to make the jump from writing, sitting at a computer writing, in your little like golem cave, to them like stepping out into the world where you're running this huge business operation that has this public facing side and it has this very pitchy presentational and budgetary side and line producers that tell you you're over budget and you go, what's a budget?
而且没有人教你如何实现跨越：从写作、坐在电脑前写作、呆在你的小哥布林洞穴里，到突然踏入世界，去运营一个庞大的商业实体，它有着面对公众的一面，有着非常强调推介、展示和预算的一面，还有执行制片人告诉你你的预算超支了，而你却说，什么是预算？

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:37.730
I just write ideas on the page and then they sort of get shot by someone somewhere.
我只是在纸上写下点子，然后它们就会被某人不知在什么地方拍出来。

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:52.970
We really had a genuinely huge learning curve there to go from writers on a staff to being showrunners and Rob, his job, and it was not suggested by us, it was suggested by the very nervous studio and the very nervous network who knew how green we were.
我们在那里确实经历了一个真正巨大的学习曲线，从团队里的编剧转变为剧集运作人，而罗布的工作——这不是我们建议的，而是非常紧张的制片厂和非常紧张的电视网建议的，因为他们知道我们有多嫩。

00:10:52.970 --> 00:11:04.370
It said, what about we pair you with somebody that can help you stay calm and stay focused, take some of the work load off and tell you what to worry about and what not to worry about and that's where Rob stepped in.
他们说，不如我们给你安排一个人，能帮你保持冷静、保持专注，分担一些工作负荷，告诉你什么该担心、什么不该担心，罗布就是这样介入的。

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:11.330
Rob, was this your first time mentoring a show runner team when you worked with Craig?
罗布，当你和克雷格合作时，这是你第一次指导剧集运作人团队吗？

00:11:11.330 --> 00:11:13.105
That is a great question.
这是一个好问题。

00:11:13.105 --> 00:11:14.028
I'm not sure.
我不确定。

00:11:14.028 --> 00:11:17.649
It probably was or maybe I had done it once before.
很可能是，或者也许我以前做过一次。

00:11:17.649 --> 00:11:20.879
I was offered this many times and I often didn't like the script.
我曾多次被邀请担任这个职务，但我通常不喜欢剧本。

00:11:20.879 --> 00:11:25.649
I was clearly when I read this that these guys were super talented, so we made my job very easy.
当我读到这个剧本时，我清楚地看到这些家伙超级有才华，所以他们让我的工作变得非常简单。

00:11:25.649 --> 00:11:27.179
And how does that come to be?
那是怎么促成的呢？

00:11:27.179 --> 00:11:30.289
Maybe in this case, I'll just say, how did this come to be?
也许在这个情况下，我直说吧，这到底是怎么促成的？

00:11:30.289 --> 00:11:35.889
How did it come to be that you were put into this role or you agreed to take on this role?
你是怎么进入这个角色的，或者说你是什么时候同意承担这个角色的？

00:11:35.889 --> 00:11:37.675
Craig, do you remember how that happened?
克雷格，你还记得那是怎么发生的吗？

00:11:37.675 --> 00:11:38.415
I don't remember.
我不记得了。

00:11:38.415 --> 00:11:40.940
Someone said, hey, are you interested in being these guys?
有人说，嘿，你有兴趣带这几个家伙吗？

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:42.290
They have a great script and...
他们有一个很好的剧本，而且……

00:11:42.289 --> 00:11:44.810
First of all, I'm glad it meant so much to you.
首先，我很高兴它对你意义重大。

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:47.009
That it sucked in your head so indelibly.
以至于它在你的脑海里刻得那么深，像个烂摊子。

00:11:47.009 --> 00:11:48.698
You're like, did I work on that one?
你当时想，我参与过那个吗？

00:11:48.698 --> 00:11:51.090
Did I help out for a sock on how I met your mother?
我有没有为《老爸老妈浪漫史》的一只袜子帮过忙？

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:52.370
I know I've seen the show.
我知道我看过这部剧。

00:11:53.970 --> 00:12:07.929
I should say Rob remained a consulting producer on the show for its entire run and is the only other director, essentially, who directed episodes, found which mother saved for a couple of other little bits and bops of other people popping in, but he directed a bunch of them to.
我应该说，罗布在整部剧的播出期间一直是该剧的咨询制片人，而且基本上是除了其他一些人偶尔客串露面的一些小零碎之外，唯一一个执导过剧集的导演，而且他还执导了其中的好几集。

00:12:07.929 --> 00:12:14.169
Rob was very involved, despite forgetting our How I Met story, which just seems like sack-relitched in this particular conversation.
罗布非常投入，尽管他忘记了我们的《老爸老妈》故事，在这个特定的对话中，这看起来简直就像是大逆不道。

00:12:14.169 --> 00:12:15.669
I remember the coffee shop.
我记得那家咖啡馆。

00:12:15.669 --> 00:12:18.149
I remember meeting you gentlemen very clearly.
我清楚地记得与你们几位见面。

00:12:18.149 --> 00:12:19.529
It was a coffee shop.
那是一家咖啡馆。

00:12:19.529 --> 00:12:20.370
I know you do.
我知道你记得。

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:21.690
It was our meet cute.
那是我们的初次相遇。

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.529
It was the inciting incident of our rom-com.
那是我们浪漫喜剧的煽动性事件。

00:12:24.529 --> 00:12:32.850
But Rob, I feel like the studio liked you, 20th, And you had done a couple of pilots with the folks at CBS and they loved you.
不过罗布，我觉得制片厂很喜欢你，20th（二十世纪福克斯）。

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:37.129
I don't know, I don't remember who suggested it, 20th or CBS, but somehow your name came along.
而且你和哥伦比亚广播公司（CBS）的人合作过几个试播集，他们非常喜欢你。

00:12:37.129 --> 00:12:39.649
I will say this, we did meet a couple of other people.
我不知道，我不记得是谁提议的了，20th还是CBS，但不知怎么你的名字就出现了。

00:12:39.649 --> 00:12:41.610
We went on a couple of other first dates, Rob.
我要说的是，我们确实见了其他几个人。

00:12:41.610 --> 00:12:42.450
Did you know that?
我们去过好几场其他的“初次约会”，罗布。

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.450
Now I hear, now it comes out, now it comes out.
你知道吗？

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:47.090
This is how you find out.
现在我听到了，真相大白了，现在真相大白了。

00:12:47.090 --> 00:12:53.970
I wanna say we probably met with like four or five other people and did not click with any one of them as much as we did you.
这就是你发现真相的方式。

00:12:53.970 --> 00:12:55.210
I guess we never told you this.
我想说我们大概见了其他四、五个人，没有一个像对你那样一拍即合。

00:12:55.210 --> 00:12:57.850
I feel very awkward about it coming out like this.
我想我们从没跟你说过这个。

00:12:57.850 --> 00:12:59.090
while it's being recorded.
我觉得这样说出来感觉有点尴尬。

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:00.929
Exactly, you just forgive it.
就在它被录音的时候。

00:13:00.929 --> 00:13:04.529
Carter and I went to a coffee shop in Brentwood, I think.
没错，你尽管原谅它吧。

00:13:04.529 --> 00:13:09.145
I think you made us come somewhere near where you lived, because you were the grownup when we were the kids, and we just did.
卡特和我去了一家布伦特伍德的咖啡馆，我想。

00:13:09.145 --> 00:13:09.809
That sounds right.
我想你让我们去离你住处不远的地方，因为你是大人而我们是小孩，我们就照做了。

00:13:09.809 --> 00:13:14.570
We listened to our elders, and we met you and I remember just loving you immediately.
听起来是这样。

00:13:14.570 --> 00:13:26.850
Everything you said about the script was what we hoped the script would make someone feel, and you loved the emotional and storytelling structural aspects of the script as much or more than the jokes.
我们听从长辈的话，我们见到了你，我记得我立刻就爱上了你。

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:29.610
you said, the jokes can come, you can add jokes.
你对剧本所说的一切，正是我们希望剧本能带给别人的感受，而且你对剧本的情感和叙事结构层面的喜爱，甚至不亚于对笑话的喜爱，甚至更多。

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:37.570
You can always add jokes, but the bones of what the heart of the story is, and the bones of the structure, like you saw that in our script, and that meant the world to me.
你说，笑话可以有，你可以加笑话。

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:40.450
You were one of the earliest believers in how I met your mother.
你随时都可以加笑话，但是故事核心的骨架，以及结构的骨架，你在我们的剧本里看出了这一点，这对我的意义重大。

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:44.769
Full disclosure, I had read three other how I met your mother's, but I picked yours.
你是《老爸老妈浪漫史》最早的坚定信仰者之一。

00:13:44.769 --> 00:13:47.250
Ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha ha.
坦白说，我读过其他三个《老爸老妈浪漫史》的剧本，但我选了你们的。

00:13:47.250 --> 00:13:49.210
We got there first, look, we moved the fastest.
哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈哈。

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:50.049
We got there first.
我们先到的，看，我们行动最快。

00:13:50.049 --> 00:13:52.690
There's a lot of lawsuits, there's still a lot of litigation happening around that.
我们先到的。

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:59.370
One of the things about being copyrighted is you help your friends, even when you're competing for the same slots.
有很多诉讼，现在仍然有很多关于这方面的诉讼在进行。

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:05.250
And often you work on other people's pilots and you help punch up and you're doing whatever you can to help and they take it or leave it.
拥有版权的一大特点是你要帮助你的朋友，即使你们在竞争相同的时段。

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:09.570
But what I've seen over the years are pilots get lost.
而且你经常参与别人的试播集，帮忙润色，尽你所能提供帮助，他们采纳或拒绝都行。

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:11.169
There's so many notes coming at you.
但我这些年看到的是试播集迷失了方向。

00:14:11.169 --> 00:14:16.250
From other writers, from the executives, from the studio, you get a barrage of notes.
有那么多的意见向你涌来。

00:14:16.250 --> 00:14:21.250
And I've seen over and over again people try to please everyone, try to Frankenstein all these notes together.
来自其他编剧，来自高管，来自制片厂，你收到了狂轰滥炸般的意见。

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:28.970
And a pilot that started great does not only not get better, but it becomes something other than that, but what's great about it gets lost.
我一次又一次地看到人们试图取悦所有人，试图把所有这些意见拼凑成一个弗兰肯斯坦。

00:14:28.970 --> 00:14:34.730
And so I felt like my job was this part, it was great, these guys are talented, make sure that it doesn't get lost.
一个原本出色的试播集不仅没有变得更好，反而变成了别的东西，而它原本的精彩之处却迷失了。

00:14:34.730 --> 00:14:36.129
That was it.
所以我觉得我的工作就是：这一部分很棒，这些家伙很有才华，要确保它不会迷失。

00:14:36.129 --> 00:14:38.250
But saying that was it, that's everything.
就是这样。

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:39.570
That's everything.
但要说这就是全部，那这就是全部。

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:43.850
Erica, so many bells are ringing about things that we have talked about.
埃里卡，有好多铃声在响，关于我们讨论过的事情。

00:14:43.850 --> 00:15:24.809
And if we're looking at this as a teacher-student type of relationship that it goes along with so much, I mean, Rob, what Erica has written about what we've been talking about, difference between my image of the straw man teacher who we're who are knocking down here all the time is you the teacher know things you want the students to repeat them back and by the way they're probably never going to use them in their lives all the way to the other end of the spectrum of we are all here kind of collaborating and learning together and it's my job as a teacher not to give you but to find in you something and help you to bring that out and that's what But I'm hearing here that Craig was looking for and what Rob was able to do.
如果我们把这看作是一种老师与学生的关系，并且它与很多东西相契合，我是说，罗布，埃里卡所写关于我们一直在讨论的东西，即我脑海中那个我们一直在批判的稻草人老师的形象——即老师你知道一些事情，你希望学生把它们重复给你听，顺便说一句，他们可能永远也不会在生活中使用它们——与光谱另一端的区别在于，我们都在这里进行某种合作，一起学习，而作为一个老师，我的工作不是塞给你东西，而是从你身上发现一些东西，并帮助你把它激发出来，这就是我所听到的克雷格当时在寻找的，也是罗布能够做到的。

00:15:24.809 --> 00:15:42.210
Can you give an example of a time that the three of you had a challenge, had an issue, had an aha in working together where the work was changed as a result of an interaction that the three of you had?
你们三个能举出一个例子吗？当你们在合作中遇到挑战、遇到问题、产生顿悟时，由于你们三个人之间的互动，工作发生了改变？

00:15:42.210 --> 00:15:43.210
A hundred percent.
百分之百。

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:48.090
What I always think about Rob, and I always hope Rob's not embarrassed being here with me calling him a mentor.
我总是想起罗布，而且我一直希望罗布不会因为我在这里称他为导师而感到尴尬。

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:55.049
It seems like the kind of thing that would make him wanna leave the interview maybe, you get too embarrassed and correct, find it cringey.
这听起来像是那种会让他想离开采访的事情，也许你会觉得太尴尬和纠正，觉得很尴尬。

00:15:55.049 --> 00:15:56.149
You are correct.
你说得对。

00:15:57.649 --> 00:16:00.049
He's staying though, he's not storming off, I see him.
不过他留下来了，他没有气冲冲地离开，我看到他了。

00:16:00.049 --> 00:16:10.690
Rob's mantra, and I carried it through all nine seasons, but it meant the most when we were just a pilot, because keep in mind when you're running a pilot, you're one of like 40 pilots the network bought that year.
罗布的座右铭，我一直把它贯彻到整整九季中，但当我们在做试播集时，它的意义最重大，因为请记住，当你制作试播集时，你是电视网那一年买下的40个试播集之一。

00:16:10.690 --> 00:16:16.370
And then out of those 40 pilots, they maybe shoot 10 of them, and then maybe they pick up two or three of them.
然后在这40个试播集中，他们可能会拍摄其中的10个，然后可能会预定其中的2个或3个。

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:18.330
So the odds are very much against you.
所以几率对你非常不利。

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:23.330
And so at each point when we kept getting to the next level of the video game, we were in disbelief.
因此，当我们一次次闯入这个电子游戏的下一关时，我们都感到难以置信。

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:28.009
And finally when it got picked up to go be a TV show, it was absolutely shocking.
最后当它被预定制作成一部电视剧时，简直是太令人震惊了。

00:16:28.009 --> 00:16:34.009
We had to be the darkest horse of that race because we were nobody and we were so young was our first pilot.
我们在那场竞赛中绝对是最不被看好的黑马，因为我们默默无闻，而且我们太年轻了，这是我们的第一个试播集。

00:16:34.009 --> 00:16:46.129
And Rob from day one said, because you'd get these notes and some of the notes are infused with some panic, I think from the network in the studio, then the notes are infused with this thing of like, let's make it a little less different.
罗布从第一天起就说，因为你会收到这些意见，其中一些意见充满了某种恐慌，我想来自电视网和制片厂，然后这些意见充满了一种态度，比如，让我们把它改得没那么与众不同吧。

00:16:46.129 --> 00:16:47.529
Let's make it a little less strange.
让我们把它改得没那么奇怪吧。

00:16:47.529 --> 00:16:49.009
This is for CBS.
这是为CBS拍的。

00:16:49.009 --> 00:16:54.009
You're trying to get on CBS sandwiched between King of Queens and two and a half men.
你正试图挤进CBS，夹在《人人都爱雷蒙德》和《好汉两个半》之间。

00:16:54.009 --> 00:16:56.970
And you're writing all this ooey gooey romance stuff.
而你却在写所有这些黏糊糊的浪漫桥段。

00:16:56.970 --> 00:17:00.250
You're shooting 83 scenes in like 22 minutes.
你在大约22分钟内拍摄了83场戏。

00:17:00.250 --> 00:17:02.809
You're jumping around with structure and time.
你在结构和时间上跳来跳去。

00:17:02.809 --> 00:17:06.210
It's narrated from the future about now.
它是从未来旁白讲述现在。

00:17:06.210 --> 00:17:08.289
Everything about our show was weird for that network.
我们节目的方方面面对于那个电视网来说都很怪异。

00:17:08.289 --> 00:17:24.662
And when we would get notes wanting to sand down what was unique about it, Rob would say to me in Carter we come into our office and close the door and you go, no one is ever going to apologize to you if you make your pilot more mediocre and more expected and traditional.
每当我们收到想要磨平其独特性质的意见时，罗布就会对我和卡特说，我们走进他的办公室关上门，你会说：如果你把你的试播集拍得更加平庸、更加符合预期、更加传统，没有人会向你道歉。

00:17:24.662 --> 00:17:27.311
And then it sucks, and it doesn't get on TV.
然后它扑街了，没能在电视上播出。

00:17:27.311 --> 00:17:34.233
You're not going to get a nice handwritten apology from the network of the studio saying, hey, sorry, that's on us.
你不会收到电视网或制片厂亲笔写的道歉信，说：嘿，对不起，那是我们的错。

00:17:34.233 --> 00:17:38.989
We overnoted you, we toned down what was special, and now it's kind of nothing.
我们给你的意见太多了，我们淡化了它的特别之处，现在它变得什么都不是了。

00:17:38.989 --> 00:17:44.948
It's like everything else, and we're going to go with this other thing with a bigger star attached.
它就像其他东西一样，我们要去做另一个挂着更大明星名号的项目了。

00:17:44.948 --> 00:17:47.115
And Rob said, you'll never get that.
罗布说，你永远不会得到那个。

00:17:47.115 --> 00:17:57.950
You have to go down swinging that this is your vision, this is your voice, this is your thing, and hold on to that like it is gold, like it is the most precious thing in the world.
你必须为这是你的愿景、这是你的声音、这是你的东西而死战到底，像抓住黄金一样守护它，把它当成世界上最珍贵的东西。

00:17:57.950 --> 00:17:59.094
And fail with that.
那就带着这个去失败吧。

00:17:59.094 --> 00:18:01.562
Don't fail with the watered down version.
不要带着被稀释的版本去失败。

00:18:01.562 --> 00:18:09.086
Fail with the version you know that you could do 200 episodes of if you're lucky enough to sneak this thing past the goalies.
带着你深知即使幸运地通过了层层关卡、能拍够200集的那个版本去失败吧。

00:18:09.086 --> 00:18:13.299
And I thought of that daily, weekly, monthly, yearly for nine seasons.
在整整九季里，我每天、每周、每月、每年都在想着这件事。

00:18:13.299 --> 00:18:17.059
And Rob was there for all of those nine seasons to keep reminding us.
而罗布经历了这全部的九季，不断提醒着我们。

00:18:17.059 --> 00:18:19.539
I basically taught them the correct way to fail.
我基本上是教会了他们正确的失败方式。

00:18:19.539 --> 00:18:21.309
That was my job.
这就是我的工作。

00:18:21.309 --> 00:18:25.549
Rob, can you say what the better way to fail is?
罗布，你能说说更好的失败方式是什么吗？

00:18:25.549 --> 00:18:27.470
Look, these guys were super talented.
听着，这群家伙超级有才华。

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:28.309
It was clear.
这显而易见。

00:18:28.309 --> 00:18:29.549
The script was great.
剧本太棒了。

00:18:29.549 --> 00:18:32.869
So I just encouraged them to trust that and go down swinging.
所以我只是鼓励他们相信这一点，放手一搏、背水一战。

00:18:32.869 --> 00:18:35.230
Go do the show you wanna do.
去做你想做的节目。

00:18:35.230 --> 00:18:39.029
Go as big as you can go and not worry about the result.
把格局放大到极致，不要去担心结果。

00:18:39.029 --> 00:18:40.630
With Craig said, that was my mantra.
正如克雷格所说，那是我的座右铭。

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:43.109
And look, if they were less talented, their show was less good.
听着，如果他们没那么有才华，他们的节目没那么好。

00:18:43.109 --> 00:18:48.470
I think I've had a different mantra, but you can tell that they knew what they were doing and the show was special.
我想我会有一套不同的座右铭，但你可以看出他们知道自己在做什么，这个节目很特别。

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:49.710
That doesn't mean it would succeed.
这并不意味着它一定会成功。

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:53.390
It doesn't mean it would get on the air, but the show was special, so it was really make your show.
这并不意味着它能登上荧幕，但这个节目很特别，所以关键真的是要做出你自己的节目。

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:55.950
Make the show you have in your head.
做出你脑海中的那个节目。

00:18:55.950 --> 00:19:00.799
And my job was to try to keep the other noise away.
而我的工作就是尽量把外界的杂音挡开。

00:19:00.799 --> 00:19:11.400
Were there times, I might use the wrong language, so forgive me, but when the studio or when the producers said to you, Rob, listen.
有没有那么几次——我用的词可能不对，请原谅我——当电视台或者制片人对你说，罗布，听着。

00:19:12.799 --> 00:19:14.079
Go fix this.
去把这个修好。

00:19:14.079 --> 00:19:16.240
I don't want to know the answer, is there?
我不想知道答案，有吗？

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:19.000
No, I never got pulled aside.
不，我从来没有被叫到一旁谈话。

00:19:19.000 --> 00:19:21.720
I heard the same notes they heard.
我听到的意见和他们听到的一模一样。

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:22.799
You swear that's true?
你发誓这是真的？

00:19:22.799 --> 00:19:25.200
I do swear that you just being nice to me.
我发誓，除非你只是在对我好。

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:28.720
I have zero memory of anything, so, but that is my memory.
我对任何事都没什么记忆了，所以，但这就是我的记忆。

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:40.200
I remember in episode, I believe it was episode two, you guys had some scene on the roof with Robin and Ted And it was kind of a sweet scene, and it was kind of an emotional scene.
我记得在某一集，我相信是第二集，你们在屋顶上拍了一场罗宾和泰德的戏，那是一场挺温馨的戏，也是一场挺感人的戏。

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:42.039
And at the end of it, everyone converged.
而在戏的结尾，所有人都聚到了一起。

00:19:42.039 --> 00:19:44.000
You gotta put jokes on it, da, da, da, da, da.
你得在上面加点笑话，啦啦啦啦啦。

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.440
And I remember very clearly pulling you guys aside and saying, this seems great.
我非常清楚地记得把你们拉到一边说，这拍得真棒。

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:48.839
Don't listen to anybody else.
不要听任何其他人的。

00:19:48.839 --> 00:19:50.240
Go downswing.
背水一战。

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:56.559
And I think that was a turning point in our relationship as well, cause I think you guys did it, and I think it changed things.
我觉得这也是我们关系的一个转折点，因为我觉得你们真的照做了，而且我觉得这改变了一切。

00:19:56.559 --> 00:19:59.190
We did do it, and we kept doing it.
我们确实照做了，而且我们一直坚持这样做。

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:11.269
Were there other aspects of show-running other than writing, where Craig, you felt like Rob mentored you, showed you the way toward being successful at that gig?
除了写作之外，在剧集运作的其他方面，克雷格，你有没有觉得罗布指导了你，为你指明了如何成功胜任那份工作的方向？

00:20:11.269 --> 00:20:13.869
Yeah, a whole bunch of different ways.
是的，在很多不同的方面。

00:20:13.869 --> 00:20:17.910
It's funny, when writers often get asked, like, how do you learn how to do that job?
说来有趣，当编剧经常被问到，比如，你是怎么学会做那份工作的？

00:20:17.910 --> 00:20:19.309
Or how do you learn how to be a show minor?
或者你是怎么学会当一个剧集运作人的？

00:20:19.309 --> 00:20:27.309
And I think what's interesting about this podcast, in general, is exploring the different ways that people learn and are mentored.
我觉得这个播客有趣的地方，总的来说，就是探讨人们学习和接受指导的不同方式。

00:20:27.309 --> 00:20:35.336
And I would say such a huge part of what Rob did for us and what I try to do to younger writers now like just telling them they're not crazy.
我想说，罗布对我们所做的事情中，以及我现在试图对年轻编剧做的事情中，很大一部分就是告诉他们，他们没有疯。

00:20:35.336 --> 00:20:40.402
Just being reassured you're not crazy for the weird unique specific thing you want to do.
只是让他们确信，对于你想要创作的那个古怪、独特、特定事物，你并没有疯。

00:20:40.402 --> 00:20:45.582
That's the thing you think is special about the thing you've created is worth holding onto.
你认为你创作的东西最特别的地方，是值得坚守的。

00:20:45.582 --> 00:20:53.608
To your question, Erica, like off of what Rob just said about episode two, jump ahead, flash forward, and how I met your mother speak, right?
回答你的问题，埃里卡，就像顺着罗布刚才说的第二集，快进，闪回，用《老爸老妈浪漫史》的话说，对吧？

00:20:53.608 --> 00:20:58.105
We played with structure, so I'll flash forward and jump ahead in the timeline.
我们玩过结构，所以我会在时间线上进行闪回和快进。

00:20:58.105 --> 00:21:13.246
To the end of season one, we had this really emotional season one ending where Ted gets the girl he finally wins over Robin and comes home to find Marshall, his best friend, sitting on their front stoop in the rain and Marshall holds up his returned engagement ring.
到了第一季的结尾，我们有了一个非常感人的第一季结局：泰德终于赢得了女孩罗宾的芳心，回到家却发现他的死党马歇尔正坐在前廊的台阶上淋雨，马歇尔举起了他退回的订婚戒指。

00:21:13.246 --> 00:21:19.622
Lily has returned and called up the engagement, returned the ring and it is the lowest point of Marshall's life.
莉莉回来了，取消了婚约，退回了戒指，这是马歇尔人生的最低谷。

00:21:19.622 --> 00:21:22.069
He's crying in the rain on his front steps.
他在前台阶的雨中哭泣。

00:21:22.069 --> 00:21:25.635
Right his head is sort of bringing this victory of getting the girl.
对吧，他的脑子里某种程度上还带着赢得女孩芳心的胜利感。

00:21:25.635 --> 00:21:30.406
It's coming home the morning after he had this magical name with Robin and it's really sad.
那是他与罗宾共度神奇一夜后的清晨回家，而这一切真的让人难过。

00:21:30.406 --> 00:21:31.245
It's really sad.
真的太让人难过了。

00:21:31.245 --> 00:21:33.290
There's like this amazing song playing.
里面还放着一首超级动听的歌。

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:35.125
It feels like a little indie movie.
感觉就像一部小型独立电影。

00:21:35.125 --> 00:21:36.802
It did not feel like a CBS show.
它一点也不像CBS的节目。

00:21:36.802 --> 00:21:38.637
It did not feel like Kim McQueen's.
它一点也不像金·麦克奎恩的剧。

00:21:38.637 --> 00:21:40.735
It did not feel like two and a half men.
它一点也不像《好汉两个半》。

00:21:40.735 --> 00:21:43.146
After that, we locked picture on that episode.
在那之后，我们那一集锁定了画面。

00:21:43.146 --> 00:21:46.712
We got called into a meeting at CBS with the studio and the network.
我们被叫去CBS与电视台和电视网开会。

00:21:46.712 --> 00:21:57.670
We should have known we were in trouble because all the parents were showing up and we were told with some alarm that the perception was, and maybe the testing showed, that we had made the show too serialized.
我们本该知道自己有麻烦了，因为所有高管都出席了，而且我们带着某种惊恐被告知，大家的看法是——也许测试结果也显示——我们的节目拍得太连贯了。

00:21:57.670 --> 00:22:03.925
The audience feels, you know, there's some focus group testing show that you have to watch too many episodes in a row.
观众觉得，你知道的，有一些焦点小组测试显示，你必须连续看太多集。

00:22:03.925 --> 00:22:05.303
There's these arcs, right?
有这些故事线，对吧？

00:22:05.303 --> 00:22:06.522
It's like a soap opera.
就像肥皂剧一样。

00:22:06.522 --> 00:22:10.286
Like, you need to make it less emotional, more stand-alone, more jilky.
就像，你需要让它少一点情感，多一点独立成集，多一点轻松搞笑。

00:22:10.286 --> 00:22:15.057
It needs to be like these other shows on CBS, and they're just more like a box of candies.
它需要像CBS上的其他节目那样，更像一盒糖果。

00:22:15.057 --> 00:22:16.753
You can eat any one at any time.
你随时可以吃任意一颗。

00:22:16.753 --> 00:22:18.290
And that was not in fairness.
说句公道话，并非如此。

00:22:18.290 --> 00:22:19.139
They were right.
他们是对的。

00:22:19.139 --> 00:22:21.471
That was not the show we were making at all.
那根本不是我们当时在拍的节目。

00:22:21.471 --> 00:22:28.627
And we got a stern talking to about like, hey, we want to see more of that box of chocolates in season two, and less of the soap opera.
我们挨了一顿严厉的训斥，大意是：嘿，我们希望在第二季看到更多那盒巧克力的感觉，少一点肥皂剧。

00:22:28.627 --> 00:22:43.629
It was a bummer because it was like, we just had finished season one, it's like running to the end of the marathon, and you get a cup of water, and it's like hydrochloric acid instead of water, you're sort of like, Oh, that was not the victory celebration at the end of the marathon.
这让人很沮丧，因为这就好像，我们刚拍完第一季，就像冲向马拉松的终点线，结果你拿到了一杯水，里面装的居然是盐酸而不是水，你就会觉得，哦，这根本不是马拉松终点时的胜利庆典。

00:22:43.629 --> 00:22:54.549
And we had this kind of turning point where we had to decide if we were going to dramatically change the show and make it more like what the big boss people giving us tons of money were saying it should be.
我们经历了一个转折点，我们必须决定是彻底改变这部剧，让它更符合那些给我们巨额资金的大老板们所说的那样。

00:22:54.549 --> 00:22:59.789
And that is a moment where Rob's advice from the pilot from throughout season one came in.
正是在这个时刻，罗布从试播集到整个第一季给予的建议派上了用场。

00:22:59.789 --> 00:23:02.990
We decided we're going to go down swinging with the show we want to make.
我们决定用我们想拍的节目背水一战。

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:06.430
And we did not change it up at all in season two.
我们在第二季完全没有做任何改变。

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:07.630
If anything, it was more serialized.
如果要说有什么不同的话，那就是故事连贯性更强了。

00:23:07.630 --> 00:23:09.190
It was more emotional.
情感更充沛了。

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:10.910
There were more arcs.
故事线更多了。

00:23:10.910 --> 00:23:17.470
And what happened was people had caught up to the show over the summer and we started getting more of an audience.
结果就是，观众在夏天追上了这部剧，我们的观众群体开始扩大了。

00:23:17.470 --> 00:23:24.910
And then years passed, and over those years, the same people that were giving us those notes were like, you know what, it's good that your show has these emotional arcs.
然后几年过去了，在这几年里，当初给我们那些意见的同一拨人却说：“你知道吗，你们的节目有这些情感故事线挺好的。”

00:23:24.910 --> 00:23:27.910
It's what's special about your show, and that's great.
“这正是你们节目的特别之处，这很棒。”

00:23:27.910 --> 00:23:33.390
We've always loved that, just like we said at the end of season one, more of that.
“我们一直都很喜欢这样，就像我们在第一季末尾说的那样，多来点这个。”

00:23:33.390 --> 00:23:50.430
But in those moments when you're being told by a room full of very serious, smart people who hold the key to the money, that they're doubting something about your game plan, those are the moments where the words of somebody like Rob and his support and belief in it, that's where mentorship means the world.
但在那些时刻，当一群非常严肃、聪明、掌握着资金大权的人对你的计划产生怀疑并告诉你时，正是这些时刻，像罗布这样的人的话语以及他对你的支持和信任，才让导师的意义变得重若千金。

00:23:50.430 --> 00:23:55.950
I would also say I had my own stuff, my own project, so I was not trying to make this mine.
我也想说，我当时有自己的事情、自己的项目，所以我并不是想把这个据为己有。

00:23:55.950 --> 00:24:01.750
I was trying desperately to make it theirs, and I think that was what was helpful.
我拼命地想让这成为他们的节目，而我认为这恰恰是最有帮助的。

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:05.549
And that's also a lot of very good writers are not good showrunners.
这也是为什么很多非常优秀的编剧并不是优秀的剧集运作人。

00:24:05.549 --> 00:24:11.029
Carter and Craig happened to be turned into great showrunners, but that often is not the case.
卡特和克雷格碰巧被培养成了伟大的剧集运作人，但通常情况并非如此。

00:24:11.029 --> 00:24:23.059
You have leadership and you have public speaking and you have to deal with budgets, and different departments, and writers, and schedules, and some writers are great writers, but not good at that.
你需要领导力、公开演讲能力，你必须处理预算、不同的部门、编剧、档期，有些编剧是出色的作家，但不擅长这些。

00:24:23.059 --> 00:24:30.859
Rob, did you have people in your past who functioned as Hollywood mentors, showrunner mentors, writer mentors?
罗布，在你的过去，有没有人扮演过好莱坞导师、剧集运作人导师、编剧导师的角色？

00:24:30.859 --> 00:24:31.980
That's a good question.
这是一个好问题。

00:24:31.980 --> 00:24:33.700
I don't think I really did.
我觉得我真的没有。

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:37.940
I mean, I had some people who helped me, like gave a script to somebody or those kind of things.
我是说，我有一些帮助过我的人，比如把剧本交给某人或者类似的事情。

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:44.740
Oh, I was on Frasier for years, and Chris Lloyd ran it, And he was basically my mentor.
哦，我在《欢乐一家亲》工作过多年，克里斯·劳埃德负责制作，他基本上就是我的导师。

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:51.380
He was a spectacular writer and I basically could not get, I tried to get everything I could out of him.
他是一位出色的编剧，我基本上恨不得从他身上榨取我能得到的一切。

00:24:51.380 --> 00:24:56.859
He's a writer's writer and that's kind of my mentor, but that was already in a, I was on staff.
他是编剧中的编剧，那可以说是我的导师，但这已经是在一个……我是剧组员工的前提下了。

00:24:56.859 --> 00:25:04.829
I was his employee, but I would credit him.
我是他的雇员，但我会把功劳记在他身上。

00:25:04.829 --> 00:25:06.029
Hey, I'm Carl Radke.
嘿，我是卡尔·拉德克。

00:25:06.029 --> 00:25:14.589
You might know me from Bravo's Summer House, but on my podcast More Life, I get your favorite reality stars off the clock and into the conversations you don't usually get to hear.
你可能通过美国精彩电视台的《夏日山庄》认识我，但在我的播客《更多生活》中，我将带你走进你喜爱的真人秀明星们下班后的生活，进行那些你平时听不到的对话。

00:25:14.589 --> 00:25:18.029
The deep stuff, the vulnerable stuff, the uncomfortable stuff.
那些深刻的话题、脆弱的话题、让人不自在的话题。

00:25:18.029 --> 00:25:23.869
I've sat down with Sierra Miller, Derinda Medley, Tom Schwartz, Lindsey Hubbard, and a whole lot more.
我曾与席亚拉·米勒、多琳达·梅德利、汤姆·施瓦茨、林赛·哈伯德等许多人深入交流。

00:25:23.869 --> 00:25:29.390
We get into relationships growing up, the messy stuff, and everything that happens when the cameras aren't rolling.
我们探讨人际关系的成长、那些混乱的经历，以及摄像机没开时发生的一切。

00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:40.819
New episodes of more life every week, listen to wherever you get your podcasts or watch on YouTube.
《More Life》每周推出全新单集，欢迎在各大播客平台收听或在 YouTube 上观看。

00:25:40.819 --> 00:25:45.500
You know what strikes me as a non, a person outside of this art form?
你知道作为一个圈外人、一个不属于这种艺术形式的人，什么让我印象最深刻吗？

00:25:45.500 --> 00:26:09.319
And this is not the first time we've heard this when we've spoken to writers in the more Hollywood style, is this concept of notes and how that, I'll call it an artifact, that artifact serves as kind of the thing around which learning and doing and knowing sort of rotates.
而且这不是我们第一次听到这种说法了，当我们与更具好莱坞风格的编剧交流时，就会听到关于“意见（notes）”的概念，以及我称之为“产物”的东西——这种产物似乎成了学习、创作和认知所围绕的中心。

00:26:09.519 --> 00:26:39.704
And the thing that's really interesting listening to you all talk is how in formal education, A, we try to teach people to critique in ways that are productive for learning and B, how oftentimes the assessments of what people learn can be at odds with how innovative teachers want learners to be engaged with their process and their product.
听你们大家聊天，真正有趣的一点是：一方面，在正规教育中，我们试图教导人们如何以对学习有建设性的方式进行批判；另一方面，人们所学内容的评估，往往与创新型教师希望学习者如何参与其过程和成果的方式相冲突。

00:26:39.704 --> 00:27:00.859
This concept of how you respond to notes not as the be-all end-all of what the judgment of the quality of your work is, but rather how do you learn to respond to notes in a way that allows you to keep the artistic integrity of what you do?
关于你如何回应修改意见——不是把它看作衡量你作品质量的终极审判，而是学习如何以一种既能保持你作品的艺术完整性又能听取意见的方式做出回应，这个概念……

00:27:00.859 --> 00:27:13.960
Seems to me a really important part of Craig, what you got out of working with Rob and Rob, what you do when you engage with other teams of writers.
在我看来，这是非常重要的一部分，克雷格，这是你从与罗布合作中得到的启发；而罗布，这也是你与其他编剧团队合作时的做法。

00:27:13.960 --> 00:27:14.460
Yeah.
是的。

00:27:14.460 --> 00:27:26.993
There are so many notes because you're getting notes on the verbal pitch of the story and then the outline of what you think the story is going to be and then a draft of the script and another draft of script and a table read and a rehearsal.
会有那么多的意见，因为你要针对故事的口头构思获得意见，然后是关于你认为故事走向的大纲，接着是剧本初稿、剧本二稿、围读和排练。

00:27:26.993 --> 00:27:29.842
You have to get good at hearing notes and taking notes.
你必须学会如何好好听取意见并接受意见。

00:27:29.842 --> 00:27:37.300
And I think another thing Rob taught me is if it's a good note, tell them it's a good note, tell them why you think it's a smart note and do it.
我想罗布教给我的另一件事是：如果这是一个好意见，就告诉他们这是一个好意见，告诉他们为什么你觉得这是一个明智的意见，然后照做。

00:27:37.300 --> 00:27:38.543
And don't get defensive.
而且不要有防御心理。

00:27:38.543 --> 00:27:48.383
There's kind of this cliche like writers just get their hackles up and you're just sort of reflexively defending against notes and then you put up this wall that keeps out the good ones too.
业界有一种老套的说法，好像编剧们总是会炸毛，本能地抗拒修改意见，结果筑起一道墙，把好的意见也挡在了外面。

00:27:48.383 --> 00:27:49.575
These are smart people.
这些人都是聪明人。

00:27:49.575 --> 00:27:59.571
I know I just said that whole story about out there were some doubts and whatever but there's a lot of money involved and there's people trying to sort of put science on to art and that's hard.
我知道我刚才讲了那么多关于当时存在种种质疑的故事，但其中涉及大量的资金，人们试图将科学强加于艺术，这很难。

00:27:59.571 --> 00:28:01.694
These same people also were really smart.
但这些人同样非常聪明。

00:28:01.694 --> 00:28:10.861
They were really smart executives at CBS and 20th who gave us really good ideas and you have to sort of sometimes you have to take the hit and go they're right about this thing.
CBS和20世纪（福斯）的高管们确实非常聪明，他们给了我们很好的建议，有时你不得不接受打击，然后承认：“在这件事上他们是对的。”

00:28:10.861 --> 00:28:14.021
They're right about something that's wrong with what I wrote.
他们指出了我写的东西里确实存在问题的地方。

00:28:13.779 --> 00:28:20.740
If you can kind of yes and play the improv game to the note, you can make the script a lot better.
如果你能顺着意见玩“是的，而且（Yes, and）”的即兴游戏，就能让剧本好很多。

00:28:20.740 --> 00:28:29.099
And then you can the next day, after you do that rewrite till two in the morning, when you are at the run through and the script's better, you say to that executive, thanks, that was a really good note.
然后第二天，在你通宵改写剧本到凌晨两点之后，当你在彩排现场看到剧本变得更好了，你就可以对那个高管说：“谢谢，那个意见真的很好。”

00:28:29.099 --> 00:28:30.339
That was smart.
那真的很明智。

00:28:30.339 --> 00:28:45.180
I think the civility and sort of lack of ego of that is something that Rob taught me to defend against notes that are gonna rip apart what's important to you about the script, don't do those, say no in a nice way, or just don't say no.
我认为这种客气以及毫无傲气的态度，正是罗布教给我的——去抵制那些会撕裂剧本中对你而言重要的东西的意见；对于那些意见，不要采纳，用委婉的方式说不，或者干脆不说不。

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:47.220
Say, we're gonna take a look at that.
可以说：“我们会考虑一下的。”

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:50.660
Then rewrite it for two days and hope that they forgot about that one.
然后花两天时间重写，希望他们把那件事给忘了。

00:28:50.660 --> 00:28:55.180
But if it's a good one, don't be an ego maniac that you can't take a good note.
但如果是个好意见，就别太自大到连好意见都听不进去。

00:28:55.180 --> 00:28:58.140
Everybody there is trying to make something better.
那里的每个人都在努力把事情做好。

00:28:58.140 --> 00:29:01.339
The intent is for it to be a success and to be a good show.
他们的目的都是为了获得成功，打造一部优秀的剧集。

00:29:01.339 --> 00:29:04.660
Your job is to curate what's a good note from a bad note.
你的工作是从好意见和坏意见中进行甄别。

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:07.099
It takes a lot of putting your ego off to the side.
这需要你把自我意识放在一边。

00:29:07.099 --> 00:29:08.700
Rob was very, very good at that.
罗布在这方面非常非常擅长。

00:29:08.700 --> 00:29:16.740
and Rob is very good at talking to the network in the studio, very good at pitching, and very good at telling us what we should worry about and what we should forget about.
而且罗布非常擅长与电视网和电影公司沟通，非常擅长提案，也非常擅长告诉我们什么该担心、什么该放下。

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:18.740
With notes, you get a lot of notes.
说到意见，你会收到很多意见。

00:29:18.740 --> 00:29:28.339
There's some great notes and you take them, there's some bad notes and you ignore them, and then there's some notes like, you know, he's drinking coffee, can he drink tea, and he's like, it doesn't matter.
有些是极好的意见，你就采纳；有些是很糟糕的意见，你就忽略；还有一些意见，比如，你知道的，他在喝咖啡，能不能改成喝茶？这种意见就无所谓。

00:29:28.339 --> 00:29:32.019
So yeah, give them that note because they are your collaborative partners.
所以，顺着他们的意见又何妨，因为他们是你的合作伙伴。

00:29:32.019 --> 00:29:43.160
So if it's a lateral move and it makes them happy, maybe you give it to them to keep the relationship alive and just don't do the bad ones and often he'll give a note and what they're saying isn't what they mean.
因此，如果这只是一个无关宏旨的微调却能让他们高兴，也许你可以满足他们，以维持合作关系，只要别采纳那些糟糕的意见就行。通常他提出意见时，他们嘴里说的并不是他们真正的意思。

00:29:43.160 --> 00:29:46.207
You often try to find what's the note underneath the note.
你经常需要去寻找意见背后的真正意图。

00:29:46.207 --> 00:29:52.093
What are they missing in a deeper sense and that's the note you address rather than whatever their fix might be.
他们在更深层次上忽略了什么？这才是你应该解决的问题，而不是他们提出的表面修改方案。

00:29:52.093 --> 00:29:55.088
So if you don't take the bad ones, notes aren't so scary.
所以，如果你不接受糟糕的意见，修改意见其实就没那么可怕。

00:29:55.088 --> 00:29:57.873
If you don't feel like you have to take the bad ones.
只要你觉得自己不必接受那些糟糕的意见。

00:29:57.873 --> 00:30:10.379
A weird sort of offshoot of this and another lesson that Rob taught me that I feel like no one tells you this as a showrunner and hopefully this will not Not only compliment Rob, but be not the thing you hear a billion times from writers.
这其中有一个奇怪的衍生现象，也是罗布教给我的另一课，我觉得作为剧集运作人（showrunner），没有人会告诉你这些——希望这不仅是对罗布的赞美，而且也不是你从编剧那里听过亿万遍的老生常谈。

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:15.039
Sometimes you also need to defend against stuff coming at you from the writer's room.
有时你还需要抵制来自编剧室内部的杂音。

00:30:15.039 --> 00:30:21.539
If you're in a writer's room doing a TV show, people will want to pitch jokes that are not right for the show.
如果你在一个编剧室制作电视剧，人们会想要提出一些并不适合这部剧的笑话。

00:30:21.539 --> 00:30:26.539
They're funny and they get laughs in the room, but there's something that you as a showrunner know.
它们很好笑，也能在房间里引起笑声，但作为剧集运作人，你心里有数。

00:30:26.539 --> 00:30:29.859
You're sort of like the conductor of this orchestra and you're just like, that was a wrong note.
你就像这个乐队的指挥，心里会想：刚才那个音符弹错了。

00:30:29.859 --> 00:30:44.900
I can't tell you why that was a wrong note, but something about that was just either it's a little bit inappropriate or throws the character under the bus in some way, it's funny in theory, but it hurts the character, it hurts the story, or it takes you emotionally out of what you're trying to get out of that particular episode.
我说不出为什么那个音符不对，但它就是有点不对劲，要么是有点不太合适，要么是以某种方式把角色给出卖了。它在理论上很好笑，但它伤害了角色、伤害了故事，或者在情感上把你试图从那一集中获得的效果给带偏了。

00:30:44.900 --> 00:30:59.579
So Rob would often say to me, like, send the writers home early, man, get all the jokes from the writers, get them written down, keep a log of them all, then send them home and finish the rewrite just you, the showrunner, and the writer, maybe one other person.
所以罗布经常对我说：“让编剧们早点回家，伙计，把编剧们所有的笑话都收集起来，记下来，建个档案，然后让他们回家，由你——剧集运作人、编剧，可能再加上另外一个人，来完成最后的重写。”

00:30:59.579 --> 00:31:12.119
calmly and quietly do that in a room that is not full of people throwing shit at the ceiling to see if it sticks or having like chair races around the office or seeing who can eat the most wasabi without vomiting or whatever's happening in the writer's room.
平静而安静地在这样一个房间里完成：这里没有一群人把各种点子像扔大粪一样往墙上砸看哪个能粘住，也没有人在办公室里玩椅子赛车，或者看谁吃芥末最多而不吐——总之就是编剧室里常有的那种混乱。

00:31:12.119 --> 00:31:18.680
And just quietly and calmly go through the script and say, at each moment, what's the best thing to do for this script and this moment?
只是安安静静地过一遍剧本，在每个节点思考：为了这个剧本和这个时刻，什么才是最好的做法？

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:23.540
And sometimes some of the hilarious jokes, even the writers are throwing out, actually aren't right.
有时候，即使是编剧们抛出的那些极其搞笑的笑话，实际上也不合适。

00:31:23.540 --> 00:31:26.700
This is me defending why how much your mother was not that funny.
这就是我在为为什么《老妈老爸浪漫史（How I Met Your Mother）》没那么搞笑进行辩护。

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:35.720
I just caught all the jokes, but it actually was not dissimilar from what Rob was telling us about notes from about notes from executives, notes from the money people.
我只是拦下了所有的笑话，但这其实和罗布之前告诉我们关于高管的意见、金主们的意见没有什么两样。

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:42.795
Sometimes you have to take a moment, take a deep breath, get in a quiet room and decide what's best for the story you want to tell.
有时候你必须花点时间，深呼吸，找个安静的房间，然后决定什么对你想讲的故事才是最好的。

00:31:42.795 --> 00:31:52.140
I would also say, like an executive, the writer pitches something that, you know, it's basically a lateral move to what you already have and it's good, but you already have.
我还要说的是，就像高管一样，编剧会提出一个点子，你知道的，这基本上跟你手里现有的东西差不多，虽然挺好，但你已经有了。

00:31:52.140 --> 00:32:00.859
Sometimes you give it to them anyway because it's a dance you want to invest the writers that they can feel like they're contributing, but you also want to protect the script and the story.
有时你还是会采用他们的点子，因为这是一场舞蹈，你想让编剧们投入进来，让他们觉得自己有所贡献，但你同时也想保护剧本和故事。

00:32:00.859 --> 00:32:03.180
But you want them to be invested, and you want them to keep pitching.
但你希望他们投入其中，并希望他们不断提出点子。

00:32:03.180 --> 00:32:05.980
If you never take their pitches, then you'll lose the writer.
如果你从来不接受他们的点子，那你就会失去这个编剧。

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:09.099
So again, that's a showrunner skill, and not a writer skill.
所以再说一次，这是制片人（Showrunner）的技能，而不是编剧的技能。

00:32:09.099 --> 00:32:11.660
Yeah, and a showrunner is kind of like a teacher.
对，而且制片人有点像老师。

00:32:11.660 --> 00:32:19.819
It is almost like a little classroom, and sometimes it is good to tell, sometimes the room why you didn't take that joke, or why that idea for the story didn't feel right to you.
它几乎就像是一个小课堂，有时告诉大家，告诉整个编剧团队你为什么不采用那个笑话，或者为什么那个故事创意让你觉得不太对劲，这其实挺好的。

00:32:19.819 --> 00:32:36.045
So you can say like, I know it's funny, but there's something else I want to set up or accomplish here for the character that it kind of undercuts or undermines, you're sort of teaching this room full of people the tone you want to go for.
所以你可以说，我知道这很搞笑，但这里还有别的东西我想为这个角色铺垫或实现，而这个笑话有点削弱或破坏了这一点，你其实是在向这满屋子的人教导你想要追求的基调。

00:32:36.045 --> 00:32:43.241
So you're trying to be a teacher yourself while also figuring it out and feeling very much like a student.
所以你试图让自己成为一名老师，同时自己也在摸索，并且非常像一个学生。

00:32:43.241 --> 00:32:44.056
A weird job.
一份奇怪的工作。

00:32:44.056 --> 00:32:55.393
But I also love Craig that you identify the concept of being a teacher as about that meta-reflection process, which I don't think all education spaces see it that way.
克雷格，我也很喜欢你将“教师”这个概念等同于这种元反思过程，我觉得并不是所有的教育空间都会这样看待它。

00:32:55.393 --> 00:33:18.611
The way that we communicate effectively with folks about what matters in our discipline is by explaining not only what they should do, but by saying, here's why we're making this choice, or here's why we're not making this choice, despite the fact that that choice has some features that you might recognize as belonging in this space, right?
我们与大家有效沟通学科中什么才是重要的是，不仅要解释他们应该做什么，还要说明，为什么我们要做出这个选择，或者为什么我们不做出这个选择，尽管这个选择具有某些你可能会认为属于这个领域的特征，对吧？

00:33:18.611 --> 00:33:20.920
I mean, equivalent in mathematics.
我的意思是，数学里也是一样的。

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:28.440
When you get to higher, more sophisticated levels of mathematics, there's lots of different ways to set up and solve a particular problem.
当你接触到更高深、更复杂的数学水平时，有很多不同的方法来建立和解决一个特定的问题。

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:41.160
And a good math teacher will communicate to students not just like, that's the right answer, or that's the wrong answer, but they'll say, ah, I see why you made this choice.
一个好的数学老师不仅会告诉学生“那是正确答案”或“那是错误答案”，他们还会说，“啊，我明白你为什么会做出这个选择。”

00:33:41.160 --> 00:33:48.079
It is true that that choice will allow you to get to this place, but here's why we're gonna make this different choice.
“确实，那个选择能让你到达这个地方，但这就是为什么我们要做出不同的选择。”

00:33:48.079 --> 00:33:54.579
I still relate to that because the weirdest thing about the job of showrunner is who gets to be right?
我依然对这一点感同身受，因为这份工作最奇怪的事情在于：谁才是对的？

00:33:54.579 --> 00:33:55.900
And are me and Carter right?
我和卡特是对的吗？

00:33:55.900 --> 00:33:59.559
Cause we created the show is the network and the studio are they right?
因为我们创造了这个节目，那电视网和制片厂是对的吗？

00:33:59.559 --> 00:34:02.119
Cause they're putting millions and millions of dollars to do it.
因为他们投了数百万美元来制作它。

00:34:02.119 --> 00:34:02.960
So they get to be right.
所以他们是对的。

00:34:02.960 --> 00:34:03.799
Are the writers right?
编剧是对的吗？

00:34:03.799 --> 00:34:05.839
Cause the writers want to be right instead of the network.
因为编剧想听编剧的，而不是电视网的。

00:34:05.839 --> 00:34:06.759
Don't listen to the network.
别听电视网的。

00:34:06.759 --> 00:34:08.000
Let's do this hilarious joke.
我们来演这个搞笑的笑话吧。

00:34:08.000 --> 00:34:09.840
It's like super dirty or whatever it is.
它超级低俗或者诸如此类。

00:34:09.840 --> 00:34:10.800
Who's right?
谁才是对的？

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:13.000
So right loses all meaning.
所以“正确”这个词失去了所有的意义。

00:34:13.000 --> 00:34:17.480
The way I tried to talk to the writer's room because I felt you have this imposter syndrome, right?
我试图跟编剧团队沟通的方式是，因为我觉得你们会有这种冒名顶替综合征，对吧？

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:20.739
because you're like, I don't know anything that much more than anyone else.
因为你会觉得自己懂的东西并没有比别人多多少。

00:34:20.739 --> 00:34:23.219
I just know what I want this show to feel like.
我只是知道我想让这个节目呈现出什么感觉。

00:34:23.219 --> 00:34:29.380
So I would always try to say like, here's why I like this one idea and here's why I don't like this other one as much.
所以我总是试图表达：为什么我喜欢这个创意，为什么我没有那么喜欢另一个创意。

00:34:29.380 --> 00:34:31.780
And I acknowledge that that is subjective.
而且我承认这是主观的。

00:34:31.780 --> 00:34:34.179
That is my subjective opinion.
那是我的主观意见。

00:34:34.179 --> 00:34:42.940
It might be great on another show and I can't even fully explain to you exactly why I'm not putting that joke in, but I'm gonna try to put it in the words and try to steer it this other way.
它在别的节目里可能会很棒，我甚至无法完全向你解释清楚我为什么不把那个笑话放进去，但我会尽量把它用语言表达出来，并试着把它引导到另一个方向。

00:34:42.940 --> 00:34:45.780
I feel like over time I got better at that.
我觉得随着时间的推移，我在这方面做得更好了。

00:34:45.780 --> 00:34:49.780
And I did feel like the writers attuned to that, they feel respected.
而且我也确实觉得编剧们能理解这一点，他们感到了被尊重。

00:34:49.780 --> 00:35:10.300
And if you kind of acknowledge, and this is what's good about the sort of story you told about teaching, it's very respectful to the student or the recipient of that sentiment to say, you're not wrong, you're not wrong, there isn't a really a writer wrong, there's just this weird specific target I'm trying to hit with this show.
如果你能承认这一点——这也是你讲的关于教学的故事的好处所在，对学生或这种观点的接受者非常尊重，去对他们说：你没有错，你没有错，编剧之间其实没有什么对与错，只有一个我试图通过这个节目击中的奇怪的具体靶子。

00:35:10.300 --> 00:35:18.699
And someday you should go do your own show, and that's the target everyone will aim at, and you'll try your damnedest to explain to them the targets over there.
总有一天你应该去做你自己的节目，那将是每个人都会瞄准的目标，你会竭尽全力向他们解释目标在那边。

00:35:18.699 --> 00:35:24.139
It may be small, it may be like high up on a tree in the woods, but that's the target we're aiming at.
它可能很小，可能就像树林里一棵树高处的某个东西，但那就是我们瞄准的目标。

00:35:24.139 --> 00:35:28.099
And that's your job is to respectfully explain that you're not right.
你的工作就是满怀尊重地解释：你并不是错的。

00:35:28.099 --> 00:35:30.059
It's just the tone you're going for.
这只是我们追求的基调而已。

00:35:30.059 --> 00:35:31.500
It's more abstract than that.
它比那更抽象。

00:35:31.500 --> 00:35:40.139
And Craig was very good at communicating, and I would also say it's self-serving in the best possible way, because it's a very hard job to run a show.
克雷格非常擅长沟通，我还要说的是，这是以最好的方式做对自己有利的事，因为制作一档节目是一项非常艰巨的工作。

00:35:40.139 --> 00:35:43.579
And you want people to learn the show and learn what you're looking for.
你希望人们了解这个节目，了解你在寻找什么。

00:35:43.579 --> 00:35:46.340
they make your life easier and you'll get more health.
他们会让你的生活更轻松，你会得到更多的帮助。

00:35:46.340 --> 00:35:48.500
And ultimately, that's what you want.
归根结底，这才是你想要的。

00:35:48.500 --> 00:35:50.380
You can't do it all on your own.
你不可能一个人完成这一切。

00:35:50.380 --> 00:36:00.099
And if you can teach them why this works and why, this doesn't work and we're all moving in the same direction, you'll get better pitches and your show will have more, you'll get more health.
如果你能教导他们为什么这个行得通、为什么那个行不通，并且我们都在朝着同一个方向前进，你就会得到更好的点子，你的节目也会收获更多，你会得到更多帮助。

00:36:00.099 --> 00:36:03.659
Can I throw a super nerd bomb into the conversation?
我能在对话里扔一颗超级书呆子炸弹吗？

00:36:03.659 --> 00:36:09.780
That's maybe my greatest gift as a podcast host is throwing a nerd bomb.
作为播客主持人，扔书呆子炸弹可能是我最大的天赋。

00:36:09.780 --> 00:36:23.500
One of the things that I'm really interested in is how we communicate complex ideas of what it means to make art, particularly with young people, but with newcomers of all ages.
我真正感兴趣的事情之一，是我们如何传达关于艺术创作意义的复杂理念，尤其是向年轻人，但也包括各个年龄段的新人。

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:31.980
And I'm a theater maker, so I think a lot about how you get people making theater, who've never made theater before.
我是一个戏剧创作者，所以我经常思考如何让那些从未做过戏剧的人开始做戏剧。

00:36:31.980 --> 00:36:48.139
And one of the big driving ideas in narrative theory that inspires me is the concept of a good story as having both what they call reportability and credibility, right?
叙事理论中启发我的一个核心理念是，一个好故事既要有所谓的“可述性”，又要有“可信度”，对吧？

00:36:48.139 --> 00:36:53.860
Where reportability is the thing that makes the story unique worth telling.
其中可述性是让故事变得独特、值得讲述的特质。

00:36:53.860 --> 00:37:06.380
And credibility being its quote unquote believability, but not believability in terms of I believe that that happened, but believability in terms of right capacity to locate it within a genre.
而可信度则是它的“所谓”可信度，但这并不是指“我相信那件事真的发生了”的那种可信度，而是指能够将其准确归类于某种类型的能力。

00:37:06.380 --> 00:37:08.300
I know what this is.
我知道这是什么。

00:37:08.300 --> 00:37:24.500
I hear a lot in all of your conversations, Rob and Craig, that the balance of reportability and credibility is what the show runner's job is in many ways.
罗布和克雷格，我在你们所有的对话中经常听到，平衡可述性和可信度在很多方面正是剧集主管的工作。

00:37:24.500 --> 00:37:28.900
And I just wonder if that resonates with you or if that makes you think of anything.
我只是想知道这是否能引起你们的共鸣，或者是否让你们想起些什么。

00:37:28.900 --> 00:37:33.500
Yeah, I really like that way of thinking about storytelling and theater and TV.
是的，我非常喜欢这种看待讲故事、戏剧和电视剧的方式。

00:37:33.500 --> 00:37:37.500
That's a great way of looking at stories, reportability and credibility.
这是一种看待故事的绝佳视角，即可述性和可信度。

00:37:37.500 --> 00:37:47.900
feels like what we were trying to have on How I Met Your Mother was maybe someone would watch our TV show on CBS and yes it would feel a little different and that would be a good thing.
感觉我们在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中努力想要达到的效果是，也许有人会在CBS观看我们的电视剧，而且确实会觉得有点与众不同，这是一件好事。

00:37:47.900 --> 00:37:59.331
It would feel a little bit young for that network, it would feel like it was being playful with storytelling and emotionally, tonally it would feel different and that was not a bad thing, that was a good thing.
它对于那个电视网来说会显得略微年轻化，会让人觉得它在叙事上很有玩味，并且在情感和基调上也会与众不同，这不是一件坏事，而是一件好事。

00:37:59.331 --> 00:38:17.133
That reportability part feels very true, like that's the absolute most precious thing to hold on to because that's you reminding yourself why you wanted to tell the story to begin with, how you would pitch, the elevator pitch of the story, boil it down to one sentence, you're going to say the reportable part of it, hopefully.
可述性那部分感觉非常真实，就像那是必须紧紧抓住的最珍贵的东西，因为那是你在提醒自己当初为什么想要讲这个故事，你将如何进行电梯演讲，将故事浓缩成一句话，希望你能说出其中值得讲述的部分。

00:38:17.133 --> 00:38:38.581
And then credibility, I think is that piece of that Rob and Pam were so good with the actors about, which was investing them in feeling like these were real human beings, and not sacrificing character to joke, not making the process of making a TV show feel unsafe and we're constantly singing for our supper and we're going it canceled, the stage of how I met your mother felt incredibly safe.
然后可信度，我认为那是罗布和帕姆在演员身上做得非常出色的一部分，也就是让他们投入到感觉这些都是真正的活生生的人中去，不为了笑料而牺牲角色，不让制作电视剧的过程让人感到没有安全感，也不用担心我们总是在为生计苦苦挣扎、随时会被砍掉。《老爸老妈浪漫史》的片场让人感到无比安全。

00:38:38.581 --> 00:38:43.590
Other actors, guest doctors would come on and they would go, what's happening on this stage?
其他演员、客串演员来到这里时会说，这个片场发生什么了？

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:45.985
I can't believe how not toxic it feels here.
我真不敢相信这里感觉一点也不乌烟瘴气。

00:38:45.985 --> 00:38:47.618
What's in the water over here?
这儿的水里放了什么？

00:38:47.618 --> 00:38:48.543
Are you guys hot?
你们脑子没热吧？

00:38:48.543 --> 00:38:50.449
We get that kind of question a lot.
我们经常听到这种问题。

00:38:50.449 --> 00:38:55.620
The credibility piece in terms of selling a piece of art that feels like it's its own universe.
在推销一个感觉自成体系的艺术作品时，可信度这个部分至关重要。

00:38:55.620 --> 00:39:03.459
The pocket we got into with how I met your mother is one in which I think we we all felt like these were real people that we really cared about.
我们在《老爸老妈浪漫史》中进入的那种状态，是我们所有人都觉得这些人是真实存在的，是我们真正关心的。

00:39:03.460 --> 00:39:11.219
When how much weather ended, I felt like friends were leaving me that I could, I felt like heartbroken that these characters, I was gonna miss the actors.
当《老爸老妈浪漫史》结束时，我觉得有朋友正在离开我，我甚至感到心碎，因为这些角色，我会想念那些演员的。

00:39:11.219 --> 00:39:12.179
They're all very nice people.
他们都是很好的人。

00:39:12.179 --> 00:39:14.340
I was really gonna miss these characters.
我是真的会想念这些角色。

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:18.699
That's the moment I realized like, I've just come to believe in them completely.
那是使我意识到我完全开始相信他们的时刻。

00:39:18.699 --> 00:39:20.179
I completely believe in these.
我完全相信他们。

00:39:20.179 --> 00:39:21.739
These are real people to me.
对我来说，这些都是真人。

00:39:21.739 --> 00:39:24.380
I cry for them, I laugh with them.
我为他们哭泣，与他们欢笑。

00:39:24.380 --> 00:39:31.980
And I think that credibility piece, you have to as a creator of art, sort of delude yourself into thinking these are almost actual real beings.
我认为关于可信度这一点，作为艺术创作者，你必须自我欺骗，认为这些几乎就是真实的生命。

00:39:31.980 --> 00:39:34.139
Like we spent nine years living in that set.
就像我们在那个布景里生活了九年。

00:39:34.139 --> 00:39:36.699
The writer's room was right next to the set of How Much Mother.
编剧室就在《老爸老妈浪漫史》片场的旁边。

00:39:36.699 --> 00:39:42.739
I would know it was Christmas in Los Angeles because our set would be decorated for our Christmas episode or our Thanksgiving episode.
我知道洛杉矶迎来圣诞节是因为我们的片场会为圣诞或感恩节单集进行装饰。

00:39:42.739 --> 00:39:54.860
And you'd go down and you'd like have a Christmas party on the side of the bar of How Much Your Mother and the reality line and the fiction line blurred for years in this wonderful way that it was like an escape.
你会走下去，在《老爸老妈浪漫史》的酒吧一角办一个圣诞派对，现实与虚构的界限多年来以一种奇妙的方式模糊了，就像是一种逃避。

00:39:54.860 --> 00:40:01.420
It was like walking out of time and out of reality into stage 22 on the Fox lot, I really entered into that credibility piece.
就像是走出了时间和现实，进入了福克斯片场的22号摄影棚，我真正融入了那种可信度之中。

00:40:01.420 --> 00:40:12.420
What you're teaching people to do, the writers, the actors, you're trying to, if it's a teacher aspect, you're trying to teach them what that universe is that we're all gonna agree is real.
你教导大家去做的——编剧们、演员们，如果你扮演的是导师的角色，你就是在教导他们那个我们都同意它是真实的宇宙是什么样的。

00:40:12.420 --> 00:40:17.980
We're gonna do this nine year improv game of pretending this is all really happening to us.
我们要进行这场长达九年的即兴游戏，假装这一切真的发生在我们身上。

00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:21.659
And if we would be shooting an emotional scene on one of the characters would be crying.
如果我们正在拍摄一场情感戏，其中一个角色在哭泣。

00:40:21.659 --> 00:40:26.380
And like the buff camera guy with like biceps bigger than my entire body would be like wiping away.
像那个肌肉比我整个身体还健硕的壮汉摄像师也会在一旁擦拭眼泪。

00:40:26.380 --> 00:40:29.500
It's like, we really all invested in that credibility piece.
这就好比，我们所有人都真正投入到了那个可信度的部分。

00:40:29.500 --> 00:40:35.300
And I think that came from the pilot, and I think that goes all the way back to Rob saying, just create the universe you want to create.
我认为这源自试播集，而且一直追溯到罗布所说的：去创造你想创造的宇宙。

00:40:35.300 --> 00:40:38.300
No one's ever going to say sorry for changing it for you.
没有人会因为为你做出了改变而对你说抱歉。

00:40:38.300 --> 00:40:39.699
And then it doesn't work.
如果那样还行不通。

00:40:39.699 --> 00:40:42.420
You have to go downswing in with what you want to make.
你必须带着你想创作的东西全力以赴。

00:40:42.420 --> 00:40:46.219
And also, Carter and Craig had a very clear vision of what they wanted to make.
而且，卡特和克雷格对他们想创作的东西有着非常清晰的愿景。

00:40:46.219 --> 00:40:50.260
It was based on their own lives, and they had a vision of the tone and the humor.
这是基于他们自己的生活，他们对基调和幽默有着明确的愿景。

00:40:50.260 --> 00:40:55.900
And again, my job was to say, it's a great vision, and stick to it, and don't get pushed off it.
再说一次，我的工作就是说，这是一个伟大的愿景，坚持下去，不要动摇。

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:59.400
My brother is a dispute negotiator.
我哥哥是一名争议谈判专家。

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:06.079
And he talks about something called the artichoke system of negotiation and really struck me.
他谈到了一种叫做“谈判的洋蓟系统”的东西，这真的震撼了我。

00:41:06.079 --> 00:41:11.360
He says, your job is to hold onto the heart, hold onto the heart and give away the leaves freely.
他说，你的工作是守住核心，守住核心，然后大方地放弃叶子。

00:41:11.360 --> 00:41:15.000
And I feel like that's how you can do all of this stuff.
我觉得这就是你如何能做到所有这一切的方式。

00:41:15.000 --> 00:41:19.280
If the actor has an idea that doesn't affect the heart, give it to him freely.
如果演员有一个不影响核心的想法，那就大方地给他。

00:41:19.280 --> 00:41:22.000
If it affects the heart, then you have to protect the heart.
如果它触及了核心，那么你就必须保护核心。

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:26.000
And I think that's the job of the show runner and director, really.
我认为这确实就是剧集主管和导演的工作。

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:31.320
That would have been a great thing to say to me years ago when I was really looking at you for advice, but great, I guess I'll take it now.
多年前当我真的很想向你寻求建议时，如果能听到这样的话就好了，不过没关系，我想我现在接受它也不迟。

00:41:31.320 --> 00:41:36.119
I did want to ask one final question, which is two parts.
我还想问最后一个问题，它包含两个部分。

00:41:36.119 --> 00:41:40.760
One, what are you both working on now?
第一，你们俩现在都在忙些什么？

00:41:40.760 --> 00:41:53.840
And if you have extended this mentorship capacity in any other ways in more recent projects, so Rob, have you been on other shows where you've been asked to serve in this role, Craig?
第二，你们是否在最近的项目中以其他方式延续了这种师徒传承的能力？所以罗布，你有没有在其他节目中被邀请担任这个角色？克雷格呢？

00:41:53.840 --> 00:41:59.960
Now that you're older than Rob, are you serving in that capacity?
既然你比罗布年龄大，你在以这种身份发挥作用吗？

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:03.239
I'm actually not doing as much TV these days.
其实我这几年做电视没那么多了。

00:42:03.239 --> 00:42:07.559
We did do a couple of other short-lived series and pilots that didn't go forth.
我们确实做过其他几部短命的剧集和未能播出的试播集。

00:42:07.559 --> 00:42:18.960
It's very hard after the joy that was nine years of getting to do your own show to sort of move out and back into the wilds, back into the wilderness of not doing that.
经历了做自己的剧集那种长达九年的快乐之后，再走出来，重新回到外面那种无依无靠的荒野中，回到不干这行的荒凉之中，是非常艰难的。

00:42:18.960 --> 00:42:21.000
So I've been writing more prose lately.
所以我最近写了更多的散文。

00:42:21.000 --> 00:42:23.639
I'm actually working on my first book.
我实际上正在写我的第一本书。

00:42:23.639 --> 00:42:42.400
So I've been doing a little more solo stuff, but all of those lessons, and in fact, writing this book, which is the biggest thing I've ever written alone, has required all of the same things we've talked about here today, including the reportability and credibility piece, and holding on to just what am I really trying to do here?
所以我一直在做更多独立的创作，但所有那些经验教训——事实上，写这本书（这是我写过最大部头的独力创作）需要用到我们今天在这里谈论的所有相同的东西，包括可述性和可信度，以及坚持“我在这里到底想做什么”？

00:42:42.400 --> 00:42:45.840
I'm gonna spend months and months trying to create this story.
我要花上好几个月的时间来构思这个故事。

00:42:45.840 --> 00:42:46.340
Why?
为什么？

00:42:46.340 --> 00:42:47.592
What's most important?
什么是最重要的？

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:48.880
What's the thing you can't let go of?
什么是你绝对不能放弃的东西？

00:42:48.880 --> 00:42:51.480
What is the thing that makes it worth still doing this?
是什么让这件事依然值得去做？

00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:56.159
And having that ability to kind of boil down that question as a writer is huge.
作为一名作家，能够把这个问题提炼出来的能力是至关重要的。

00:42:56.159 --> 00:43:02.199
And I really owe a lot of that to Rob, that way of looking at the heart of the artichoke, I guess.
我真的在很大程度上要感谢罗布，感谢那种看待洋蓟核心的方式吧。

00:43:02.199 --> 00:43:05.800
For me, I have a bunch of shows that I am doing currently.
对我来说，我手头正在做一堆节目。

00:43:05.800 --> 00:43:14.840
And some of them I'm also trying to do shows where I help create a show and write the first few and launch it, but hand it off.
其中有些节目我也在尝试创作，即我帮忙打造一个节目并写前几集，然后将其交接出去。

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:16.400
And that's not so easy to do.
这并不是那么容易做到的。

00:43:16.400 --> 00:43:17.800
I'm trying to do that as well.
我也在努力做到这一点。

00:43:17.800 --> 00:43:25.400
So in some level, that is some of the same kind of mentorship stuff, but more in the creator part at first.
所以在某种程度上，这确实包含了一些相同的导师传承内容，但更多是在最初的创作者阶段。

00:43:25.400 --> 00:43:31.559
It's also not so easy to do because you end up sticking with it because you fall in love with it and all that.
这也不是那么容易做到，因为你最后往往会因为爱上它而对其恋恋不舍，诸如此类。

00:43:31.559 --> 00:43:34.559
Well, thank you both so much for joining us today.
好吧，非常感谢你们两今天能参加我们的节目。

00:43:34.559 --> 00:43:41.800
Craig, I guess if you could not that it's your job to pull lessons from this, that's what Eric and I do, but you went through a lot of school.
克雷格，我想如果你能——虽然从这里总结教训不是你的工作，那是埃里克和我做的事，但你经历了很多学校教育。

00:43:41.800 --> 00:43:46.920
You have now been mentored and you have acted as this mentor.
你现在接受过指导，也扮演过这种导师的角色。

00:43:46.920 --> 00:43:55.280
And you say, what for you is the best way that you learned in coming to this new job where you clearly learned?
你会说，对于进入这份显然让你学到了东西的新工作，你所经历的最佳学习方式是什么？

00:43:55.280 --> 00:43:56.280
It worked.
它成功了。

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:57.280
You did it.
你做到了。

00:43:57.280 --> 00:43:58.280
Congratulations.
恭喜。

00:43:58.280 --> 00:43:59.280
It worked very well.
效果非常好。

00:43:59.280 --> 00:44:00.280
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:01.280
But what's the heart of it?
但核心是什么？

00:44:01.280 --> 00:44:03.329
What do you need from someone like Rob?
你从像罗布这样的人身上需要什么？

00:44:03.329 --> 00:44:11.969
How I met your mother was very autobiographical as my wife would tell you when she would watch it on Monday night and go like, I can't believe you put an argument on National Television.
正如我妻子在周一晚上看这部剧时所说，《老爸老妈浪漫史》非常有自传色彩，“我真不敢相信你把一场争吵搬到了全国电视上。”

00:44:11.969 --> 00:44:13.690
That was a piece I never got better at.
那是这方面我从未有所长进的一点。

00:44:13.690 --> 00:44:16.010
Rob, you never took me aside and told me that one.
罗布，你从来没有把我拉到一边跟我说过那个。

00:44:16.010 --> 00:44:20.250
You should ask before you take the personal details and put them in front of millions people.
你在把个人隐私拿出来放在数百万人面前之前应该先问一声的。

00:44:20.250 --> 00:44:22.409
I was just terrible about that the whole time.
我整个过程在这方面简直糟透了。

00:44:22.409 --> 00:44:33.730
But that idea of mining your own life to create your art, like really being unafraid to dig into the messiest parts of your own life and try to set that tone.
但那种从你自己的生活中汲取灵感来创作艺术的想法，就像是真正无所畏惧地去挖掘你生活中最糟糕的部分，并努力定下这个基调。

00:44:33.730 --> 00:44:52.809
If you're teaching a room of writers, you have to be able to dig into your own messiest stuff and put it on the table and go like, this story doesn't make me look good, but I'm going to tell it in the writer's room, I'm going to talk about my breakup, and we're going to put our characters through some hell, and I want everybody in this room to just put it on the table.
如果你在教导一屋子的编剧，你必须能够挖掘你最糟心的事情，把它摆在桌面上说：这个故事并不能让我显得光彩照人，但我要在编剧室里把它讲出来，我要谈谈我的分手，我们要让我们的角色经历一些地狱般的考验，我希望这个房间里的每个人都把它坦诚地摆在桌面上。

00:44:52.809 --> 00:44:54.530
This will be our therapy session.
这将是我们的心理治疗课。

00:44:54.530 --> 00:44:55.889
Let's all just put it out there.
让我们都把它倾诉出来吧。

00:44:55.889 --> 00:44:58.690
Let's talk about the feeling of losing a parent.
让我们来谈谈失去父母的感觉。

00:44:58.690 --> 00:45:06.170
Let's have a very real, very therapeutic conversation, and we will see what felt true in our conversation, and we will write a script that hopefully feels like that.
让我们进行一次非常真实、非常有疗愈效果的对话，看看我们对话中哪些部分感觉是真实的，然后我们来写一个希望能带来这种感觉的剧本。

00:45:06.170 --> 00:45:08.409
And Rob is a really great voice from minute one.
罗布从第一分钟起就是一个非常棒的声音。

00:45:08.409 --> 00:45:10.010
He said, I know the script is you guys.
他说，我知道剧本就是你们。

00:45:10.010 --> 00:45:11.369
I can see you in it.
我能在里面看到你们的影子。

00:45:11.369 --> 00:45:19.409
The Marshall and Billy character were me and my wife, Rebecca, and Ted was based on Carter, and Rob was always a great voice of like, just hold on to that.
马歇尔和比利的角色就是我和我太太丽贝卡，而泰德是以卡特为原型的，罗布一直是一个很好的声音，就像是，只要坚持这一点。

00:45:19.409 --> 00:45:22.050
That is what it is, and always follow that.
这就是它本身的内核，永远追随它。

00:45:22.050 --> 00:45:25.730
That's what I try to do in my writing now, and that's what I try to teach that writing staff to do.
这也是我现在写剧本时努力在做的事，也是我试图教编剧团队去做的事。

00:45:25.730 --> 00:45:29.929
I think you can always feel that in writing, and TV, and art, and theater, and film, whatever.
我觉得你总能在写作、电视、艺术、戏剧、电影以及各种事物中感受到这一点。

00:45:29.929 --> 00:45:33.010
You can always feel when there's stakes, because it's exciting.
你总能感受到什么时候是有利害关系的，因为这很令人兴奋。

00:45:33.010 --> 00:45:34.289
It's like watching a high wire act.
就像在看高空走钢丝。

00:45:34.289 --> 00:45:36.570
You can feel like something's on the line here.
你能感觉到这里有些东西悬于一线。

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:42.530
Someone's putting it out there, but again, tell the people in your life before you steal all those details and put it on national TV.
有人在倾其所有，但话说回来，在你把那些细节全偷走并搬上全国电视之前，先告诉你的生活中的那些人。

00:45:42.530 --> 00:45:46.530
If there's one takeaway from this conversation, please do that.
如果说这次对话能有什么收获，请务必这么做。

00:45:46.530 --> 00:45:54.530
And I would say as a mentor, find two guys who are immensely talented, terrific, lovely people who don't need much help.
我想说，作为导师，去找两个才华横溢、了不起、惹人喜爱且不需要太多帮助的家伙。

00:45:54.530 --> 00:45:57.530
And that's a great way to go about being a mentor.
这也是成为一名导师的好方法。

00:45:57.530 --> 00:46:11.829
For all the folks listening who are educators in any way, for me, the lesson is start everything with what your kids are bringing into the learning space.
对于所有在收听节目、身为各类教育工作者的朋友们，对我和而言，这个教训是：一切都要从孩子们带入学习空间的那些东西开始。

00:46:11.829 --> 00:46:21.489
It is so difficult in our modern educational ecology to focus on kids' assets and gifts.
在我们现代的教育生态中，去关注孩子们的优势和天赋是如此困难。

00:46:21.489 --> 00:46:40.750
And in part, I think what makes what you do so difficult, Craig, is because we unlearn through most of our young life that our stories, our experiences, our lives are relevant and valuable in those public spaces.
部分原因，克雷格，我认为正是因为你的工作如此艰难，因为我们在大部分的年轻岁月中都在学着遗忘，忘记我们的故事、我们的经历、我们的生活在那些公共空间里是具有相关性和价值的。

00:46:40.909 --> 00:46:54.230
For all of you who listen, who work with young people, who are educators, this is a reminder to always begin with what kids are bringing into your learning space.
对于所有在收听节目、与年轻人共事、身为教育工作者的朋友们，这是一个提醒：永远从孩子们带入你们学习空间的东西开始。

00:46:54.230 --> 00:46:56.829
Yeah, I'm gonna add one last thing to that.
是的，我要在最后补充一点。

00:46:56.829 --> 00:47:06.469
One other thing that Rob was amazing with was there was a time in the middle of how much a mother, where Rob was going through something really hard, a health thing that he went through that was really hard.
罗布非常了不起的另一个方面是，在《老爸老妈浪漫史》的中期，罗布经历了一段非常艰难的时期，是一件很艰难的健康问题。

00:47:06.469 --> 00:47:08.269
And I was going through something really hard.
而我也经历了一段非常艰难的时期。

00:47:08.269 --> 00:47:12.750
I became the parent of a child with health differences, learning differences.
我成了一个有健康差异、学习差异的孩子的家长。

00:47:12.750 --> 00:47:16.550
And it was like really hard while I was writing this comedy, right?
在我写这部喜剧的时候，这真的是太难了，对吧？

00:47:16.550 --> 00:47:18.469
Your job was to go in and write jokes.
你的工作是进去写笑话。

00:47:18.469 --> 00:47:24.389
And Rob was really the best person for me to talk to in the middle of all that.
在这一切当中，罗布真的是我倾诉的最佳人选。

00:47:24.389 --> 00:47:27.030
He had been through some hard things and I was going through some hard things.
他经历过一些艰难的事情，而我也正在经历一些艰难的事情。

00:47:27.030 --> 00:47:29.269
They weren't the exact same hard things.
它们不完全是同一件艰难的事情。

00:47:29.269 --> 00:47:34.630
But Rob was a wonderful voice of support and saying, take that and find a way to put it on the show.
但罗布给予了极好的支持，他说，把这些经历拿来，想办法把它放到节目中去。

00:47:34.630 --> 00:47:36.610
Like, find some of that emotion.
比如，找到其中的一些情感。

00:47:36.610 --> 00:47:38.829
And yes, you're feeling a little bit outside of it all right now.
是的，你现在感觉自己有点游离于这一切之外。

00:47:38.829 --> 00:47:39.670
Maybe we both are.
也许我们两个都是。

00:47:39.670 --> 00:47:43.409
Maybe we both feel a little outside of the normal flow of life looking in.
也许我们两个都觉得自己在俯视生活，游离于正常的生活轨迹之外。

00:47:43.409 --> 00:47:44.829
But there's a way to use that.
但有一种方法可以利用这一点。

00:47:44.829 --> 00:47:47.989
And also, I'm here to talk to you about that stuff.
而且，我在这里陪你聊这些事。

00:47:47.989 --> 00:47:52.030
In some way, I feel like that stuff did make it on how I met your mother.
在某种程度上，我觉得那些东西确实进入了《老爸老妈浪漫史》。

00:47:52.030 --> 00:48:01.670
Some of those emotions and some of the sort of like darker, more nuanced tones of that show, like some of the stuff we were really going through, sort of led to some different emotional terrain on the show.
其中一些情感，以及该剧一些较为黑暗、更具细微差别的基调，比如我们真正经历的一些事情，最终促成了该剧一些不同的情感领域。

00:48:01.670 --> 00:48:06.070
Not literal one-to-one apples to apples, but it influenced the show.
不是字面上一对一完全契合，但它确实影响了这部剧。

00:48:06.070 --> 00:48:11.590
So that idea of just even the incredibly hard things you're going through, you can use that.
所以，即便是你正在经历极其艰难的事情这种想法，你也可以利用它。

00:48:11.590 --> 00:48:17.070
And you can also just acknowledge that yes, you're writing a comedy and living a drama right now.
并且你也可以承认，是的，你现在正写着喜剧，却过着戏剧般的生活。

00:48:17.070 --> 00:48:18.230
And that's okay.
这没关系。

00:48:18.230 --> 00:48:19.829
And you're gonna feel that way for a little while.
有一段时间你会这种感觉。

00:48:19.829 --> 00:48:23.190
You're gonna feel outside of it, but you'll get through it.
你会觉得自己游离在外，但你会挺过去的。

00:48:23.190 --> 00:48:28.909
And Rob was, I don't know, I just wanted to add that Rob was an amazing voice of that for me in the middle of that show.
罗布，我不知道，我只是想补充一点，在那个节目的制作过程中，罗布对我来说是一个了不起的精神支柱。

00:48:28.909 --> 00:48:30.030
Well, and you were for me.
嗯，你对我也一样。

00:48:30.030 --> 00:48:32.909
And Craig is one of my best dearest friends in the world.
而且克雷格是我在世上最好、最亲爱的朋友之一。

00:48:32.909 --> 00:48:37.349
So the relationship has grown, and that's, can't ask for more than that.
所以我们的关系加深了，这，夫复何求。

00:48:37.349 --> 00:48:41.429
And I also feel the best, if you don't cry in a comedy, it's not a great comedy.
我也觉得最好的是，如果你在喜剧里没有哭，那就不是一部伟大的喜剧。

00:48:41.429 --> 00:48:44.670
So I like a, a, a, a tearful comedy.
所以我喜欢一、一、一、一出让人流泪的喜剧。

00:48:44.670 --> 00:48:46.150
Hey, man, that's the dream.
嘿，老兄，这才是梦想。

00:48:46.150 --> 00:48:47.989
Uh, who's the better show winner?
呃，谁才是更好的剧集运作人？

00:48:47.989 --> 00:48:50.269
Me or John Wells, real quick, just to wrap up.
我还是约翰·韦尔斯，简单问一下，马上就要结束了。

00:48:50.269 --> 00:48:54.110
We'll, we'll, we'll go offline.
我们会，会，会私下讨论。

00:48:54.110 --> 00:48:57.010
Thanks Rob, thanks Craig.
谢谢罗布，谢谢克雷格。

00:48:57.010 --> 00:49:00.030
It's so nice to get to spend a little time with both of you.
很高兴能和你们俩共度一段时光。

00:49:00.030 --> 00:49:00.869
Thank you.
谢谢你们。

00:49:00.869 --> 00:49:01.710
It's a pleasure.
我的荣幸。

00:49:01.710 --> 00:49:02.550
Thank you guys for having us.
谢谢你们邀请我们。

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:03.550
It's been fantastic.
太棒了。

00:49:03.550 --> 00:49:04.550
Thank you.
谢谢。

00:49:04.550 --> 00:49:11.380
What a great thing.
多么棒的事啊。

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:21.300
Alec, two ideas that jumped out at me wearing my arts educators hat that I wanted to just quickly reflect with you on.
亚历克，带着我艺术教育工作者的帽子，有两个想法让我眼前一亮，我想和你快速交流一下。

00:49:21.300 --> 00:49:46.639
The first is the concept of collaboration and not only how much they talked about collaboration, but how collaboration as an outcome, which is something I talk a lot about in my book, through really clearly in the way these folks not only worked with one another, but talked about what it meant to be a good showrunner.
首先是合作的概念，不仅在于他们谈论了多少合作，而且合作作为一种成果——这也是我在书中经常谈论的事情，从这些人不仅如何共事、而且如何谈论成为一个优秀的剧集运作人这意味着什么的具体方式中，清晰地展现了出来。

00:49:46.639 --> 00:49:48.159
So many examples.
有太多的例子了。

00:49:48.159 --> 00:50:05.400
I loved some of the talk about how when you're getting ideas from writers, from execs, sometimes you take those ideas so that they are contributing positively toward the product.
我很喜欢他们的一些谈话，讲到当你从编剧、高管那里获得意见时，有时你接受这些意见是为了让他们为成品做出积极的贡献。

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:14.639
Even if you maybe have a better idea or you maybe have a simpler idea because the collaboration in some sense is the point.
即使你可能有更好的主意，或者你可能有一个更简单的点子，因为从某种意义上说，合作本身就是重点。

00:50:14.639 --> 00:50:22.480
And that is something I think that schools and formal educational settings could take up really meaningfully in their own practice.
我认为学校和正规教育环境可以在他们自己的实践中非常有意义地采纳这一点。

00:50:22.480 --> 00:50:23.480
Absolutely.
绝对是的。

00:50:23.480 --> 00:50:27.119
And in this case, it's sort of both creative and political, right?
在这种情况下，它既有点像创作上的，又有点像政治上的，对吧？

00:50:27.119 --> 00:50:33.909
Because when an executive at a studio or a network is giving you a suggestion, they are your boss.
因为当制片厂或电视网的高管给你提建议时，他们是你的老板。

00:50:33.909 --> 00:51:07.349
paying for your show, they also know that it's your show and you know the lines are drawn there but of course it's a great idea to basically I think most people in this world the basic rule is say yes as much as you can because you're going to say no sometimes you're going to have reasons for saying no and the hope is that the perfect relationship is that an executive is giving you notes with the understanding that you are the best person to decide if that works for your show or not that's I'm saying this is the very positive version of all of this.
他们为你的节目出资，他们也知道这是你的节目，你知道界限在哪里，但当然，提出建议是个好主意，基本上我认为这个世界上大多数人的基本规则是：尽可能多地说“好”，因为有时你得说“不”，你会有说“不”的理由，而人们希望达到的完美关系是，高管在给你提意见时，理解你是决定这是否适合你的节目的最佳人选，我是说，这是所有这一切非常积极的一面。

00:51:07.349 --> 00:51:12.469
You have to do this, no, I don't want to do this, and then you've got a problem on your hands.
你必须这样做，不，我不想这样做，然后你就有麻烦了。

00:51:12.469 --> 00:51:18.710
But in terms of collaboration, I mean, you really have to picture what a writer's room is really like.
但就合作而言，我的意思是，你真的必须想象一下编剧室到底是什么样子的。

00:51:18.710 --> 00:51:28.710
I mean, these shows that when you watch them, there's a credit at the beginning for writer, but all those other credits, the story editors and various producing credits, everyone's a writer.
我是说，当你观看这些节目时，开头会有编剧的署名，但所有那些其他的署名，故事编辑和各种制片署名，每个人都是编剧。

00:51:28.710 --> 00:51:33.869
And they would literally sit in the room of 12 to 15 people writing together.
他们会字面意义上地坐在一个12到15人的房间里一起写作。

00:51:33.869 --> 00:51:38.389
Line by line, someone, you get together, you kind of come up with the idea for the show.
一句接一句，大家聚在一起，有点像构思节目的点子。

00:51:38.389 --> 00:51:47.909
Someone goes off and writes a first draft and then you sit line by line and you have all these great minds just trying to do better, trying to have something funnier or something more interesting or something different.
某个人会去写初稿，然后大家逐句地坐在一起，汇聚所有这些伟大的头脑，只是为了做得更好，为了让内容更有趣、更有料或更与众不同。

00:51:47.909 --> 00:51:51.389
And it is true, true collaboration.
这就是真正的、真正的合作。

00:51:51.389 --> 00:51:55.429
I love the idea of classroom as writer's room.
我喜欢把教室当作编剧室的想法。

00:51:55.429 --> 00:52:12.150
I feel like someday we could try to ask the question of what if you model the classroom after a writer's room and how would that change the way everyone in the space contributed to not only the collective, but also what would that mean for their own learning?
我觉得总有一天我们可以尝试问这样一个问题：如果你把教室塑造成编剧室会怎样？空间里的每个人为集体做出贡献的方式会发生怎样的改变，这对于他们自身的学习又意味着什么？

00:52:12.150 --> 00:52:17.150
It's also interesting how you say that because you have to have someone making the final decision.
你这么说也很有趣，因为你必须得有人来做最终决定。

00:52:17.150 --> 00:52:19.269
And in this case, Craig was making the final decision.
在这种情况下，克雷格在做最终决定。

00:52:19.269 --> 00:52:22.230
You're sitting around a table, a lot of ideas are being thrown around.
你们围坐在桌旁，提出了很多点子。

00:52:22.230 --> 00:52:25.869
And when Craig says, this is the one we're doing, that's the one we're doing.
当克雷格说，这就是我们要做的这个，那这就是我们要做的那个。

00:52:25.869 --> 00:52:32.829
has a very different feel from a dictatorial teacher who says, I'm right because I'm the teacher.
这与那种独裁型老师的感觉截然不同，那种老师会说：“我是对的，因为我是老师。”

00:52:32.829 --> 00:52:35.240
Listen to me because these are the rules.
“听我的，因为这就是规矩。”

00:52:35.240 --> 00:52:44.762
In this case, I mean granted they're all adults, but still what can be learned from the reason one person is making a decision because that's how things get done.
在这种情况下，当然他们都是成年人，但从一个人做决定的原因中依然可以学到东西，因为事情就是这样完成的。

00:52:44.762 --> 00:52:47.114
Because 15 people can't make a decision.
因为15个人是无法做出决定的。

00:52:47.114 --> 00:52:51.581
15 people can contribute and someone needs to do it and you want them there.
15个人可以贡献力量，但需要有人来拍板，你希望那个人存在。

00:52:51.581 --> 00:52:54.109
You don't want to just be going in circles.
你不想只是原地打转。

00:52:54.109 --> 00:52:57.929
You want someone to to say, okay, this is it, let's move forward.
你希望有人说，好吧，就是这个了，我们向前推进吧。

00:52:57.929 --> 00:53:17.320
We could talk about this forever, but I wanna do one more idea that popped out at me, which we did talk about in the episode with them, but I wanted to circle back to this idea of notes and notes as an artifact around which learning and feedback happens.
我们可以永远谈论这个，但我还想讨论另一个让我眼前一亮的点子，我们在与他们的单集节目中确实谈过这一点，但我想回过头来探讨意见（notes）这个概念，以及作为学习和反馈围绕其发生的载体的意见。

00:53:17.320 --> 00:53:59.780
Again, how much I think that practice now, we heard some about how that can be a struggle a challenge, and as you say, there's politics involved, but the idea that there exists a set of artifacts around which people can discuss the improvement of their own work also seems to me like a practice that classrooms and other learning environments could use to think about how creative work of all kinds doesn't just have to be creative writing, but creative production of all kinds could change over time for learners as they share their work with others.
再次说明，我认为这种实践现在，我们听到了一些关于这如何可能成为一种挣扎、一种挑战，正如你所说，其中涉及政治，但围绕着一组可以让人讨论如何改进自己作品的载体存在的这种想法，在我看来也是一种教室和其他学习环境可以利用的实践，思考各种创意的作品——不一定只是创意写作，而是各种创意的产出——如何随着学习者与他人分享他们的作品而随时间发生改变。

00:53:59.780 --> 00:54:01.699
Yeah, no sort of disease.
是的，这是一种“疾病”。

00:54:01.699 --> 00:54:04.119
They're an epidemic in Los Angeles, in Hollywood.
它们在洛杉矶、在好莱坞泛滥成灾。

00:54:04.119 --> 00:54:16.460
It's almost hard to hand someone a script or an idea and have them not reply with notes, which is very strange because you can imagine plenty of artists who want other people to see their stuff.
你把剧本或点子递给别人，而他们不回复你意见，这几乎是很难做到的，这非常奇怪，因为你可以想象有大量艺术家希望别人看到他们的作品。

00:54:16.460 --> 00:54:19.460
And I don't need your, I need you to tell me to make it better.
我不需要你的……我需要你告诉我怎么把它改得更好。

00:54:19.460 --> 00:54:21.699
I'm showing it to you because it's a work of art.
我把它展示给你是因为这是一件艺术品。

00:54:21.699 --> 00:54:23.820
So that's it, that's all I want from you.
所以就这样，这就是我从你那里想要的全部。

00:54:23.820 --> 00:54:26.739
But here it's odd, it's almost odd.
但在这里这很奇怪，几乎可以说是怪异。

00:54:26.739 --> 00:54:50.219
But in the context that they're talking about where they are handing scripts into a studio in a network and getting notes back, I would say the learning here to be done is from the classroom, not from the studio side, the business side, that is notes are often given without the best understanding of how one might implement this So what the creator's intent was, right?
但在他们谈论的语境中，也就是他们将剧本交给制片厂或电视台并收到修改意见时，我认为这里可以借鉴的是课堂上的经验，而不是制片厂或商业层面的经验，因为这些修改意见往往是在并不完全理解创作者如何去落实、创作者的初衷是什么的情况下给出的，对吧？

00:54:50.219 --> 00:54:52.059
That's what a good note sees.
这正是一个好的修改意见所能看到的。

00:54:52.059 --> 00:54:56.760
I see your intent and let's see how we can all make it better.
我理解你的初衷，让我们看看大家如何能把它做得更好。

00:54:56.760 --> 00:55:05.039
Well, and I would say something else I talk about a lot in my book is the importance of an authentic audience for the work that young people produce.
嗯，而且我要说的是，我在书中经常谈到的另一件事，就是对于年轻人创作的作品而言，拥有一个真实的受众至关重要。

00:55:05.039 --> 00:55:16.880
One of the reasons for the importance of an authentic audience is because it gives learners kids a grounding for why they make the decisions they make.
强调拥有真实受众的重要性，原因之一在于它为学习者即孩子们做出各种决定的依据奠定了基础。

00:55:16.880 --> 00:55:28.000
the notes in some sense could be ought to be maybe sometimes are about the reflection back of how is the audience going to take this?
从某种意义上说，修改意见本该、或者说有时确实应该反映出这样一个问题：受众会如何看待这一点？

00:55:28.000 --> 00:55:34.559
How can we make the intention as clear for the potential audience as possible?
我们该如何让潜在受众尽可能清晰地理解创作初衷？

00:55:34.559 --> 00:55:51.400
And feedback in a learning setting is given for completeness, for correctness, but not for this purpose of saying, Here's what I think you're trying to say here, and let me tell you how an audience is gonna take that.
而在学习环境中，给出反馈是为了追求完整性和正确性，而不是为了说：“这就是我认为你在这里想表达的意思，让我来告诉你受众会如何看待它。”

00:55:51.400 --> 00:56:04.360
Completely, and I should also differentiate, I did a little bit between sort of the official notes like that, and when any of us who are writing, making movies, anything like that, especially movies, they're such a huge beast.
完全是这样，我也应该区分一下，我刚才稍微区分了一下那种官方的修改意见，以及我们这些写东西、拍电影或者从事任何此类创作的人——尤其是电影，它们就像一个庞然大物。

00:56:04.360 --> 00:56:08.039
You're not just painting one thing on a piece of paper, there's so many moves to it.
你不仅仅是在一张纸上画一样东西，其中有太多的环节和变动。

00:56:08.039 --> 00:56:16.719
And in the movie that I made, I mean, Craig himself, Craig watched the film, and then said, You know, I think you should cut the last five minutes.
在我拍的那部电影里，克雷格本人，克雷格看了这部电影，然后说：“你知道的，我觉得你应该把最后五分钟剪掉。”

00:56:16.719 --> 00:56:19.639
Now, that's a huge comment.
你看，这是一个影响重大的意见。

00:56:19.639 --> 00:56:25.260
The amount of time and money it took to shoot the pieces that went into the end of it, but he was completely right.
为了拍摄最后那部分内容所花费的时间和金钱可想而知，但他完全是对的。

00:56:25.260 --> 00:56:27.360
And I absolutely could not see it myself.
而且我自己绝对看不出这一点。

00:56:27.360 --> 00:56:39.599
He knew what we wanted, he knew what we were going for, he knew what the feeling was without that ending, and with it, and he said, it's funny, but I don't think it's doing what you wanna do, and what a note, what a sort of shocking note.
他懂我们想要什么，他懂我们的目标是什么，他知道没有这个结局和有这个结局时的感受有何不同，他说：“这挺有意思的，但我觉得它没有达到你想要的效果。”这是一个多么震撼的修改意见啊。

00:56:39.599 --> 00:56:42.440
And I knew it was right the second he said it.
就在他说的那个瞬间，我就知道他是对的。

00:56:42.440 --> 00:56:44.760
There was absolutely no like, hmm, I should look at this.
绝对没有什么“嗯，我得考虑一下”这种犹豫。

00:56:44.760 --> 00:56:46.760
I was like, light switch.
我当时的感觉就像电灯开关被按亮了一样。

00:56:46.760 --> 00:56:56.199
And so, yeah, feedback that really sees what you want and helps just from another point of view, another creator's point of view is invaluable.
所以，是的，那种真正看懂了你的诉求，并从另一个角度、另一个创作者的角度提供帮助的反馈，是极其宝贵的。

00:56:56.199 --> 00:56:58.519
Oh, that response, no notes.
哦，那个回应是：无可挑剔。

00:56:58.519 --> 00:56:59.760
Well done.
干得漂亮。

00:56:59.760 --> 00:57:00.920
Perfect.
完美。

00:57:00.920 --> 00:57:12.239
Hey, Alec, as we were talking, I was pondering a question that I have for you as we wrap up our amazing conversation today with Robin Craig.
嘿，亚历克，在我们交谈的时候，我一直在琢磨一个问题，等下我们要结束今天与罗宾和克雷格的精彩对话时，我想问问你。

00:57:12.239 --> 00:57:21.840
Give me three of the best notes that you could possibly imagine getting about your life.
请说出关于你的人生，你所能想象到的最好的三个修改意见。

00:57:21.840 --> 00:57:22.679
Oh.
噢。

00:57:22.679 --> 00:57:24.000
Or your work.
或者关于你的工作。

00:57:24.000 --> 00:57:26.639
My work is fine, my life needs notes.
我的工作挺好的，我的人生才需要修改意见。

00:57:26.639 --> 00:57:30.050
Not everything that you wear needs to be blue.
你穿的衣服不一定非得全是蓝色的。

00:57:30.050 --> 00:57:31.570
One.
第一。

00:57:31.570 --> 00:57:34.400
Ice cream is not a meal.
冰淇淋可不是正餐。

00:57:34.400 --> 00:57:35.719
Two.
第二。

00:57:35.719 --> 00:57:39.559
I see you disagree with that, but I feel it would help me help.
我看得出你不同意这一点，但我感觉这会对我有帮助。

00:57:39.559 --> 00:57:42.960
You know you only live 15 minutes from the beach.
你知道你离海滩只有15分钟路程吗？

00:57:42.960 --> 00:57:44.840
Go to the beach.
去海滩吧。

00:57:44.840 --> 00:57:47.239
Three things.
三件事。

00:57:47.239 --> 00:57:49.039
Good notes.
好建议。

00:57:49.039 --> 00:57:51.320
No notes to those notes also.
对这些建议也表示：无可挑剔。

00:57:51.320 --> 00:57:58.199
Erika, what are three things that people can do to interact, to enjoy, to love, to help to support this podcast?
埃丽卡，人们可以通过哪三件事来互动、享受、热爱、帮助并支持这个播客？

00:57:58.199 --> 00:58:05.199
First of all, friends, we are so excited about all the work we're doing, and we know you love it, and we want more people to love it, too.
首先，朋友们，我们对我们所做的一切工作感到无比兴奋，我们也知道你们很喜欢它，而且我们希望更多的人也能喜欢它。

00:58:05.199 --> 00:58:13.559
So please go to the platform that you are listening to this podcast on and rate and review our podcast.
所以，请前往你收听这个播客的平台，为我们的播客评分并留下评论。

00:58:14.360 --> 00:58:15.559
One.
第一。

00:58:15.559 --> 00:58:19.199
Send us an email if you have ideas for guests.
如果你有关于嘉宾的人选想法，请给我们发电子邮件。

00:58:19.199 --> 00:58:30.599
If you just wanna tell us how much you enjoyed learning about show running, we would love to hear from you at contact at artseducatorspodcast.com.
如果你只是想告诉我们你有多么喜欢了解制片人的工作，我们非常期待听到你的声音，邮箱是 contact at artseducatorspodcast.com。

00:58:30.599 --> 00:58:31.989
Two.
第二。

00:58:31.989 --> 00:58:33.869
Wanna learn more about what we're doing?
想了解更多关于我们在做的事情吗？

00:58:33.869 --> 00:58:37.269
Wanna learn more about some of the guests you've seen?
想更多地了解你见过的某些嘉宾吗？

00:58:37.269 --> 00:58:40.030
Wanna just be part of our community?
只想成为我们社区的一部分？

00:58:40.030 --> 00:58:47.630
We have some interesting information available on our website, that's www.artseducatorspodcast.com.
我们的网站上有一些有趣的信息，网址是 www.artseducatorspodcast.com。

00:58:48.349 --> 00:58:49.969
Three things.
三件事。

00:58:49.969 --> 00:58:53.400
Onward, together.
携手并进，勇往直前。

